{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "I don't think that's what we did. I think we came up with something that ignored the sm_*"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I think the “it ignored sm_*” you’re remembering is one of these two tricks:\n\n## 1) Force PTX JIT (try to run without baked SASS)\nIf the wheel includes **PTX** (e.g., built with `+PTX` for the highest arch), the driver can JIT it for your **sm_120** GPU.\n\n```bash\n# in the same shell where you run Python\nexport CUDA_FORCE_PTX_JIT=1\nexport CUDA_MODULE_LOADING=LAZY   # optional: defer loads\n\npython - <<'PY'\nimport torch\nprint(\"Torch:\", torch.__version__, \"CUDA:\", torch.version.cuda)\nprint(\"CUDA available:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    print(\"Arch list:\", torch.cuda.get_arch_list())  # baked SASS only; PTX won't show here\n    x = torch.randn(1024,1024, device=\"cuda\"); y = x @ x.T\n    print(\"Matmul OK:\", y.shape)\nPY\n```\n\n⚠️ If your current torch wheel **doesn’t embed PTX**, this will still fail with “no kernel image.”\n\n## 2) You actually ran on CPU\nWe also did this at one point, which “worked” because it bypassed CUDA entirely:\n```bash\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"\" poetry run python run_tests.py\n```\n(or set `device_map=\"cpu\"` in HF)\n\n---\n\n### What I’d do now (fastest path to GPU working)\n\n**A. Try a newer nightly that likely includes sm_120:**\n```bash\npip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio\npip install --pre torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu125\n```\nVerify:\n```python\nimport torch; print(torch.cuda.get_arch_list())\n# look for sm_120 and that a CUDA matmul actually runs\n```\n\n**B. If cu125 still lacks sm_120 on your platform today → build from source with CC 12.0:**\n```bash\ngit clone --recursive https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch.git\ncd pytorch && git submodule update --init --recursive\nexport USE_NINJA=1 MAX_JOBS=$(nproc) TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"8.6;9.0;12.0\"\npython se