{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "pip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio\npip install --pre torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu125\nFound existing installation: torch 2.7.0.dev20250310+cu124\nUninstalling torch-2.7.0.dev20250310+cu124:\n  Successfully uninstalled torch-2.7.0.dev20250310+cu124\nWARNING: Skipping torchvision as it is not installed.\nWARNING: Skipping torchaudio as it is not installed.\nLooking in indexes: https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu125\nERROR: Could not find a version that satisfies the requirement torch (from versions: none)\nERROR: No matching distribution found for torch"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yep — the cu125 nightly you tried just isn’t published (yet) for your platform/Python, so pip can’t find it. And the cu124 nightly you *do* have was built **without sm_120** kernels — that’s why it still throws “no kernel image… (supports sm_50…sm_90)”.\n\nAt this point you’ve got two reliable choices:\n\n---\n\n## Option A (quickest that actually works today): build PyTorch from source with `sm_120`\nThis guarantees support for your RTX 5070 Laptop (CC **12.0**).\n\n**Prereqs (Ubuntu/Debian-ish):**\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install -y build-essential git cmake ninja-build python3-dev\n# You need CUDA toolkit >= 12.5 installed (driver already present).\n# If you don't have /usr/local/cuda-12.x, install NVIDIA's CUDA 12.5 toolkit first.\n```\n\n**Build & install (inside your Poetry venv):**\n```bash\n# 0) In your venv\npoetry shell\n\n# 1) Get source\ngit clone --recursive https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch.git\ncd pytorch\ngit submodule sync\ngit submodule update --init --recursive\n\n# 2) Speed up the build\npip install -U pip setuptools wheel cmake ninja typing-extensions filelock sympy\n\n# 3) Tell torch to compile for your GPU\nexport USE_NINJA=1\nexport MAX_JOBS=$(nproc)\n# Include others if you want; 12.0 is the key one for your GPU\nexport TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"8.6;9.0;12.0\"\n\n# (Optional) also embed PTX for forward-compat JIT:\n# export TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"8.6;9.0;12.0+PTX\"\n\n# 4) Build the wheel (takes a while)\npython setup.py bdist_wheel\n\n# 5) Install the built wheel\npip uninstall -y torch\npip install dist/torch-*.whl\n```\n\n**Verify:**\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport torch\nprint(\"Torch:\", torch.__version__, \"CUDA:\", torch.version.cuda)\nprint(\"CUDA available:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    print(\"Device:\", torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\n    print(\"CC:\", torch.cuda.get_device_capability(0))\n    print(\"Arch list (compiled):\", torch.cuda.get_arch_list())\n    x = torch.randn(1024,1024, device=\"cuda\"); y = x @ x.T\n    print(\"Matmul OK:\", y.shape)\nPY\n```\nYou should **no longer** see the “supports sm_50…sm_90” warning, and the matmul should succeed.\n\n> Tip: skip `torchvision/torchaudio` unless you truly need them; they would also need building (or a matching nightly) and aren’t needed for LLMs.\n\n---\n\n## Option B (temporary): run on CPU (to keep moving)\nThis lets you continue testing your code while CUDA support catches up:\n```bash\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"\" poetry run python run_tests.py\n# or in HF: pipeline(..., device_map=\"cpu\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## “Didn’t we ‘ignore’ sm_* earlier?”\nThe trick you’re recalling is **forcing PTX JIT**:\n```bash\nexport CUDA_FORCE_PTX_JIT=1\n```\nThat only works if your wheel **contains PTX** for a high-enough virtual arch. The nightly you installed doesn’t, so the driver had nothing to JIT — hence the same “no kernel image” error.\n\n---\n\n## Keep Poetry from undoing your fix\nAfter you install your custom torch wheel, add a loose pin so Poetry won’t “repair” it:\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[tool.poetry.dependencies]\ntorch = \">=2.7.0.dev0\"\n```\nThen:\n```bash\npoetry lock --no-update\n```\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can give you a single copy‑paste bash script that: checks for CUDA toolkit, clones PyTorch