{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "we got it back. I'm going to run pip freeze to get a requirements.txt list"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Nice 🎉 glad you got it working again!  \n\nYep — grabbing a snapshot with `pip freeze` is the right move. That way you’ve got a “known good” environment you can always roll back to.\n\nFrom inside your Poetry venv:\n\n```bash\n# dump to requirements.txt\npip freeze > requirements.txt\n```\n\nThat file will now include the exact wheel you built/installed, e.g.:\n\n```\ntorch==2.7.0.dev20250310+cu124\ntransformers==4.xx.x\n...\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🔐 A couple of tips\n- **Keep both files**:  \n  - `requirements.txt` → exact versions (for re-create or debugging).  \n  - `pyproject.toml` + `poetry.lock` → normal Poetry workflow.  \n- If you want to make sure Poetry doesn’t override your hand-installed torch, pin it in `pyproject.toml` to the line you see in `requirements.txt`:\n  ```toml\n  [tool.poetry.dependencies]\n  torch = \"2.7.0.dev20250310+cu124\"\n  ```\n- Then run:\n  ```bash\n  poetry lock --no-update\n  ```\n  That locks the custom version without pulling a different one.\n\n---\n\n⚡ Would you like me to show you a quick one-liner to “recreate this exact environment” later (using your requirements.txt inside Poetry), so you can clone it on Rose or another machine?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
