{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Allowed UNC Hosts\nA set of UNC host names (without leading or trailing backslash, for example 192.168.0.1 or my-server) to allow without user confirmation. If a UNC host is being accessed that is not allowed via this setting or has not been acknowledged via user confirmation, an error will occur and the operation stopped. A restart is required when changing this setting. Find out more about this setting at https://aka.ms/vscode-windows-unc."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Right — that’s the **`security.workspace.trust.allowedUNCHosts`** setting in VS Code.\n\nHere’s how it works and how to use it:\n\n---\n\n### 🔐 **What this setting does**\n\nIt lets you specify a list of **trusted network *hostnames*** (not full paths).  \nOnce added:\n\n- **VS Code will automatically trust anything you open from that host**  \n- You **won’t get prompts** like *“Update UNC hosts…”*\n- You **won’t be blocked** from running tasks/extensions/etc.  \n- You **must restart VS Code** after editing it for the change to take effect\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **How to configure it**\n\n1. Press `Ctrl+Shift+P` → **Preferences: Open Settings (JSON)**\n2. Add or edit this section:\n\n```json\n\"security.workspace.trust.allowedUNCHosts\": [\n    \"192.168.0.12\",\n    \"my-nas\",\n    \"devserver\"\n]\n```\n\n> **Important:** *No backslashes (`\\\\`)* — just the hostname or IP.\n\n3. Save → **close and re-open VS Code**.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Example\n\nIf you open a folder from this UNC path:\n\n```\n\\\\192.168.0.12\\projects\\llm\n```\n\nThen you want this in your settings:\n\n```json\n\"security.workspace.trust.allowedUNCHosts\": [\n    \"192.168.0.12\"\n]\n```\n\n---\n\nWant me to pull your current UNC path and tell you exactly what to paste in?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "why is \"poetry install\" downgrading things?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That’s expected behavior — because `poetry install` **locks your environment to match the versions recorded in your `poetry.lock` file**, *even if newer versions exist*.\n\nSo if you run:\n\n```bash\npoetry install\n```\n\nand Poetry sees that the current environment has **newer packages** than what’s in your **`poetry.lock`**, it will *downgrade* them to exactly match the locked versions.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Why this happens\n\nPoetry treats your `poetry.lock` file as the *source of truth* for repeatable installs.\n\n| Command                | What it does                                     |\n|------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| `poetry add PACKAGE`   | Resolves and writes the **newest compatible version** into both `pyproject.toml` and `poetry.lock` |\n| `poetry install`       | Uses **existing versions in `poetry.lock`** — downgrades or upgrades your env to match it exactly |\n| `poetry update`        | Refreshes `poetry.lock` to the **latest compatible versions**, then installs |\n\n---\n\n### ✅ What to do if you *want the newer versions*:\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\npoetry update\n```\n\nThat will:\n- Re-resolve dependencies to the newest versions allowed by your version spec\n- Update `poetry.lock`\n- Then install those into your environment (instead of downgrading)\n\n---\n\n### ✨ TL;DR\n\n- **`poetry install`** = strict sync to lockfile (can downgrade)\n- **`poetry update`** = refresh lockfile (can upgrade)\n\nWant me to show you how to selectively update *just one package*, or force Poetry to always pull **latest versions**?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "now why is \"poetry update\" downgrading things?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "If `poetry update` is still *downgrading* packages, it almost always means **your version constraints in `pyproject.toml` are too restrictive** — so even though Poetry *tries* to fetch fresher packages, it is forced to resolve to **older versions** that satisfy those constraints.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Typical causes\n\n| Constraint in `pyproject.toml`     | What it means                           | Result                             |\n|------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| `\"package = \"~1.2\"`                | Only versions in the **1.x** line        | New 2.0 release will be *ignored*   |\n| `\"package = \">=1.0,<1.5\"`          | Locked between 1.0 and 1.4.x             | If you installed 1.6 manually, Poetry will **downgrade** back into allowed range |\n| `\"package = \"==1.2.3\"`             | *Exact* version pin                      | Poetry will always use that version |\n\n---\n\n### ✅ What to check\n\nOpen your `pyproject.toml` and look at the dependency specifiers:\n\n```toml\n[tool.poetry.dependencies]\ntorch = \"^2.1.0\"\ntransformers = \">=4.31,<4.32\"\n```\n\n- If you want Poetry to be free to upgrade to *any new release*, change version pins to `*` or widen them.\n- To allow *any minor/patch updates*, use `^`.\n- Then run `poetry update` again.\n\n---\n\n### 🔧 Example fix\n\n**Before (locking too low):**\n\n```toml\ntransformers = \">=4.33,<4.34\"\n```\n\n**After (allow major upgrades):**\n\n```toml\ntransformers = \"^4.33\"\n```\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to **inspect your pinned ranges** and suggest the correct specifiers so Poetry stops downgrading unexpectedly? (If so — paste your `[tool.poetry.dependencies]` block.)"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "how about giving me the .toml to take all the latest versions?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Sure — here’s a **minimally-pinned `pyproject.toml`** that tells Poetry to always pull the *latest version available* for each library (i.e. no upper or lower bounds, so nothing gets downgraded).\n\n---\n\n### 📄 **pyproject.toml**\n\n```toml\n[tool.poetry]\nname = \"oss20b_test\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Local runner for openai/gpt-oss-20b\"\nauthors = [\"Todd McCaffrey\"]\n\n[tool.poetry.dependencies]\npython = \">=3.10,<4.0\"\n\ntorch = \"*\"\ntransformers = \"*\"\naccelerate = \"*\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"poetry-core>=1.7.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"poetry.core.masonry.api\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### ⚙️ What this does:\n\n- `torch = \"*\"` → install **latest** stable release of PyTorch\n- `transformers = \"*\"` → install **latest** HuggingFace Transformers\n- `accelerate = \"*\"` → same\n- Poetry won’t **downgrade** unless a version becomes *incompatible with your Python version*.\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 To apply:\n\n1. Replace the `[tool.poetry.dependencies]` block in your existing `pyproject.toml` with the one above.\n2. Run:\n\n   ```bash\n   poetry lock --no-update   # re-generate lockfile using new loosened specs\n   poetry update             # pull everything latest\n   ```\n\n---\n\nWant me to include **bitsandbytes**, **peft**, or **flash-attention** too so you're ready for LLM fine-tuning?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "yes, add them, too, please."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here’s an upgraded dependencies block you can drop straight into your `pyproject.toml` to give you a **fully-equipped LLM training + fine-tuning stack**, always pulling the *latest* versions available:\n\n```toml\n[tool.poetry.dependencies]\npython = \">=3.10,<4.0\"\n\n# Core\ntorch = \"*\"\ntransformers = \"*\"\naccelerate = \"*\"\n\n# LLM tooling\nbitsandbytes = \"*\"\npeft = \"*\"\ndatasets = \"*\"\ntrl = \"*\"\nflash-attention = \"*\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ What this gives you:\n\n| Library         | Purpose                                       |\n|----------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `torch`         | Core PyTorch                                   |\n| `transformers`  | HuggingFace inference/fine-tuning              |\n| `accelerate`    | Multi-GPU / distributed helpers                |\n| `bitsandbytes`  | 8-bit / 4-bit quantization support             |\n| `peft`          | LoRA / QLoRA / PEFT fine-tuning                |\n| `datasets`      | HuggingFace datasets loader                    |\n| `trl`           | SFT / PPO / DPO / RLHF training utils          |\n| `flash-attention` | NVIDIA-optimized attention kernels (much faster training/inference) |\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 After pasting that in your TOML, run:\n\n```bash\npoetry lock --no-update\npoetry update\n```\n\nWould you like me to go ahead and give you a **starter `train.py` script** next — wired for LoRA fine-tuning on `gpt-oss-20b` using those libraries?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
