{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "one of the things i hate about poetry is that it's atomic in action."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yeah — that’s one of the main complaints people have with Poetry:\n\n> **It does everything in one big transaction or nothing at all** — which makes it feel *slow*, opaque, and hard to tweak mid-stream.\n\n### Why it behaves that way:\nPoetry’s whole philosophy is built around *reproducibility* — so it tries to:\n- resolve **all** dependencies at once,\n- lock them into a single snapshot (`poetry.lock`),\n- and only then install.\n  \nWhich means:\n- You *can’t* install one package at a time without re-locking everything.\n- If one tiny dependency conflicts → the **whole update or install fails**.\n- There’s no `pip install foo`-style quick hit inside a managed Poetry project.\n\n### Workarounds you *can* use:\n\n| Trick                     | Result                                        |\n|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `poetry add PACKAGE`     | Install *just one package* interactively (does rewrite lockfile though) |\n| `--no-interactions` flag | Speeds up scripted installs                    |\n| Keep a temp `venv`       | Use pure `pip` there for ad-hoc experiments, then migrate results to Poetry only when happy |\n| Use *`poetry lock --no-update`* + manual `pyproject.toml` edits | Gives some manual control back |\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can give you a **workflow** that uses:\n- A loose “scratch” virtualenv for experimentation (`pip install whatever`)\n- And then a Poetry project for clean logging / packaging *only once things work*\n\n…So you don’t get Poetry’s overhead during R&D.  \nWould that help?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
