{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Command 'nvcc' not found, but can be installed with:\nsudo apt install nvidia-cuda-toolkit"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That’s expected — **`nvcc` (the CUDA compiler) is *not installed* inside your WSL Ubuntu**, which is perfectly normal unless you explicitly installed the full CUDA toolkit.\n\n- `nvidia-smi` comes from the **Windows driver** → confirms your GPU is visible and CUDA support is present ✔️\n- `nvcc` comes from the **developer toolkit** (`nvidia-cuda-toolkit`) → and is *not required just to run PyTorch* ❌\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 So what does this mean?\n\n| Tool       | Purpose                           | Required? for PyTorch inference |\n|-----------|------------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| `nvidia-smi` | Driver + runtime check (Windows side) | ✅ Yes                          |\n| `nvcc`     | Compiler for building custom kernels | ❌ Not required                 |\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Conclusion**\nYou're not missing anything — PyTorch can still run on GPU *without* `nvcc`.\n\nThe only thing we’re fighting now is that **your PyTorch wheel doesn’t contain kernels for Ada SM=120 GPUs**.\n\n---\n\n### Shall we go ahead and try the **CUDA 11.8 nightly build** of PyTorch (`cu118`)?\n\nThis is currently your best shot at working GPU support under WSL until the official 50-series builds land.\n\nLet me know and I’ll give you the exact install command block again."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
