{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what does sm120 stand for"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "`sm_120` is NVIDIA’s way of identifying the **GPU architecture** your card is based on.\n\n- **“sm”** stands for **Streaming Multiprocessor** — the fundamental compute unit inside an NVIDIA GPU.\n- The number after it is the **compute capability version** (hardware generation and feature set).\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Examples:\n\n| CUDA “sm” code | Architecture | GPU series                          |\n|----------------|-------------|--------------------------------------|\n| `sm_50`        | Maxwell     | GTX 900-series                       |\n| `sm_70`        | Volta       | Tesla V100                           |\n| `sm_75`        | Turing      | RTX 20-series                        |\n| `sm_86`        | Ampere      | RTX 30-series                        |\n| `sm_89 / sm_90`| Hopper      | H100, GH200                          |\n| ❗`sm_120`      | Ada Lovelace| **RTX 50-series (like your 5070)**    |\n\n---\n\n### 🔧 Why this matters\n\nWhen PyTorch (or any CUDA program) is built, it includes pre-compiled kernels only for the `sm_*` architectures it knows about.  \nIf your GPU’s `sm` version **isn’t included**, PyTorch will *refuse to run kernels* on that GPU — and you get the warning you’ve been seeing.\n\nThat’s why we need a build of PyTorch **compiled with support for `sm_120`**.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to show you where to *check NVIDIA’s table of SM codes* by GPU model — or are you good to proceed with the custom build script?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
