   from torch.torch_version import __version__ as __version__\n  File \"/home/todd/dev/oss20b_test/pyTorch/pytorch/pytorch-2.5.1/torch/torch_version.py\", line 4, in <module>\n    from torch.version import __version__ as internal_version\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch.version'"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You're almost there — the issue now is:\n\n> **You’re trying to run `setup.py` from inside the *extracted tarball’s subfolder* _without initializing the git submodules_.**  \n\nThe tarball expands into a path like:\n\n```\npytorch/pytorch-2.5.1/\n```\n\n…but since this isn’t a full Git repo (no `.git` folder), the build system *still expects you to pull in all the `third_party` code via `git submodule update --recursive`*, and fails when it can’t.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Two ways you can proceed cleanly:\n\n---\n\n### ➤ **Option A — use the GitHub tarball *as a git repo***  \nClone the tag instead of downloading the tarball:\n\n```bash\ngit clone --recursive --branch v2.5.1 https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch.git\ncd pytorch\n```\n\nThat gives you the code **with all submodules initialized**, which the build system expects.\n\nThen run your build with all the env vars exactly as before.\n\n---\n\n### ➤ **Option B — if you want to continue from the tarball you extracted:**\n\nFrom **inside `pytorch-2.5.1/`**, run:\n\n```bash\ngit init\ngit submodule update --init --recursive\n```\n\nThat bootstraps a `.git` folder and pulls in all missing third-party code.\n\nThen re-run `python setup.py bdist_wheel`.\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Recommendation:\n\nUse **Option A** (git clone directly at tag) — it’s cleaner and avoids weird tarball corner cases.\n\nWant me to paste you a *single clean rebuild command sequence* using `git clone --branch v2.5.1 --recursive`, configured for CUDA+WSL?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
