{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "What's the timing on the new release"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You're in the home stretch — and you're not alone asking this question.\n\n---\n\n##  What's the timeline for full **sm_120 (Blackwell/Ada)** support in PyTorch?\n\n### 1. **Nightlies have had it since early 2025**\nPyTorch maintainers confirmed in January 2025 that **sm_120 support was already included in nightly builds** with CUDA 12.8 citeturn0search0turn0search10.\n\nA working example:\n\n```bash\npython -c \\\n  \"import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.cuda.get_arch_list())\"\n# Output shows sm_120 supported citeturn0search0\n```\n\nLater confirmations show that **nightly + CUDA 12.8 binaries** support Blackwell across Linux (including WSL2) citeturn0search10turn0search6.\n\n### 2. **Stable releases fully supported by mid–2025**\n- PyTorch **2.7.0** (and 2.7.1) with CUDA 12.8 stable builds **do** include sm_120 support in both nightly and stable versions citeturn0search5turn0search12turn0search6.\n- Containers from NVIDIA (like `pytorch/pytorch:2.7.0-cuda12.8`) have also offered this since early 2025 citeturn0search6turn0search8.\n\n### 3. **As of August 2025 ...**\n- All **stable releases (2.7, 2.8)** and nightlies built with **CUDA 12.8+** officially **support Ada/Blackwell GPUs** citeturn0search5.\n- That means any new release or binary after mid-2025 should *just work* with sm_120.\n\n---\n\n## ​ TL;DR Timeline\n\n| Timeframe        | PyTorch Status                             |\n|------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| Early 2025       | Nightly CUDA 12.8 builds added sm_120 kernel support |\n| Mid-2025         | PyTorch 2.7.0 (stable) + CUDA 12.8 → full Blackwell support |\n| August 2025      | All new stable/nightly CUDA 12.8+ builds support sm_120 out of the box |\n\n---\n\n###  What to do now\n\nIf you’re encountering compatibility issues:\n- Make sure you're installing a **CUDA 12.8 build of PyTorch** — not older CUDA 12.1/12.6 wheels.\n- Use the **2.7.0+** or **nightly** builds with `cu128` designation, e.g.:\n\n```bash\npip install torch --pre --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu128\n```\n\nThat’s the fastest way to guarantee Ada-sm_120 support without additional building.\n\nShall I check your current install and help you switch to the correct build if needed?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "We got a 93.9 C temp reading somewhere while we were working."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "93.9 °C under load *during your PyTorch/CUDA building session* is **high but not dangerous** — especially for a laptop CPU/GPU crunching full-load for an extended period.\n\n---\n\n### 🔥 How to interpret that number\n\n| Temp range      | Meaning during heavy compile/stress tasks |\n|-----------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| **≤ 85 °C**     | Totally comfortable                        |\n| **85–95 °C**    | Normal for turbo clocks under sustained load |\n| **95–100 °C**   | Hot — nearing throttle limits              |\n| **100 °C+**     | Thermal cutoff zone                        |\n\n→ So **93.9 °C** means *you were pushing the system hard*, but you're **still within expected thermal headroom** — modern chips will start throttling at ~95–98 °C before they actually thermal-limit.\n\n---\n\n### 👀 Suggestion going forward\n\n- Once PyTorch is finally working, let’s run a quick **real-world “inference” stress test**, not a compile, and see **where temps stabilize**.\n- If you're regularly brushing up against 95 °C in *normal* model usage, I can walk you through:\n  - undervolting / power-limit tuning\n  - raising fan curves\n  - shallow undervolt\n  - or defaulting to Torch inference at **lower precision (fp16/bf16)** to cut power draw 30–40%\n\n---\n\nWant me to prepare a **GPU-inference temp test script** for once we get your `torch.cuda` working again?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
