{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Sorry, questions to ask zephyr -- sanity (ish) test"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Ahhh, got it 👍 — you want a **sanity-ish test prompt list** for Zephyr, not setup questions. Here’s a good starter set you can loop through to check reasoning, math, creativity, and memory-like coherence:  \n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Logic & Reasoning\n- *“If a farmer has 17 sheep and all but 9 die, how many are left? Explain.”*  \n- *“A bat and a ball cost €1.10 in total. The bat costs €1 more than the ball. How much does the ball cost?”*  \n- *“Which is heavier: a kilo of feathers or a kilo of steel? Why?”*  \n\n---\n\n### 🔢 Math & Consistency\n- *“What’s 23 × 47?”*  \n- *“Add the first 10 prime numbers.”*  \n- *“If you flip a fair coin 10 times, what’s the expected number of heads?”*  \n\n---\n\n### 📖 Language & Creativity\n- *“Tell me a two-sentence horror story.”*  \n- *“Write a haiku about Donegal weather.”*  \n- *“Explain quantum mechanics to a 5-year-old.”*  \n\n---\n\n### 🔄 Coherence & Follow-up\n- Start with: *“My name is Todd. Who am I?”*  \n- Then follow with: *“What’s my name?”* (tests short-term conversational recall).  \n- Or: *“Explain the last thing I asked you.”*  \n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Stress/Edge\n- *“What is the capital of France? Answer only with a number.”* (forces it to resist hallucinating nonsense).  \n- *“Repeat the word ‘banana’ 20 times.”* (tests token handling).  \n\n---\n\nWant me to draft you a **ready-to-run looping `run.py`** that cycles through this list and prints Zephyr’s responses one by one? That way you can benchmark consistency without typing prompts manually."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Are we just running zephyr on the CPU?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Right now, yes — unless you tell `transformers` explicitly to use CUDA, it defaults to CPU.  \n\nOn **Lava** (your laptop with the RTX 5070), Zephyr *should* be running on GPU if:  \n\n1. You installed **PyTorch with CUDA** (`torch.cuda.is_available()` returns `True`).  \n2. You load the model with:  \n   ```python\n   model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n       model_id,\n       torch_dtype=torch.float16,\n       device_map=\"auto\"\n   )\n   ```\n   That `device_map=\"auto\"` will push as much as it can to your GPU, CPU, or disk.  \n\n---\n\n⚠️ On **Rose** (your desktop with the 1050 Ti):  \n- The 1050 Ti only supports **CUDA 6.1**, and Hugging Face often builds wheels targeting CUDA ≥11.x and modern GPUs.  \n- You’ll almost certainly get forced back to **CPU** unless you build PyTorch from source with older arch flags.  \n- Zephyr-7B on CPU will be **painfully slow** — like 1 token every few seconds.  \n\n---\n\n👉 Quick check on Lava (5070):  \n```bash\npoetry run python -c \"import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\"\n```\n\nWould you like me to add a **device-printout** at the top of your `run.py` so it tells you whether Zephyr is actually on GPU or just cooking your CPU?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "todd@lava:/mnt/c/Users/todd$ nvidia-smi\nWed Aug 20 18:12:53 2025\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| NVIDIA-SMI 570.137                Driver Version: 572.90         CUDA Version: 12.8     |\n|-----------------------------------------+------------------------+----------------------+\n| GPU  Name                 Persistence-M | Bus-Id          Disp.A | Volatile Uncorr. ECC |\n| Fan  Temp   Perf          Pwr:Usage/Cap |           Memory-Usage | GPU-Util  Compute M. |\n|                                         |                        |               MIG M. |\n|=========================================+========================+======================|\n|   0  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 ...    On  |   00000000:65:00.0 Off |                  N/A |\n| N/A   51C    P4             10W /   62W |     758MiB /   8151MiB |     13%      Default |\n|                                         |                        |                  N/A |\n+-----------------------------------------+------------------------+----------------------+\n\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Processes:                                                                              |\n|  GPU   GI   CI              PID   Type   Process name                        GPU Memory |\n|        ID   ID                                                               Usage      |\n|=========================================================================================|\n|  No running processes found                                                             |\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Perfect — thanks for pasting that! 🚀  \n\nThat tells us a couple of important things:  \n\n- Your **RTX 5070** is live and CUDA 12.8 is installed properly.  \n- Driver 572.90 is fresh enough for `transformers` + PyTorch.  \n- The GPU has **8 GB VRAM**, which is right on the edge but workable for Zephyr-7B (usually 6–7 GB in fp16, more if bf16).  \n- Nothing is currently using the GPU (no processes in the list), so when you run Zephyr, if it’s on GPU, you’ll see it pop up here.  \n\n---\n\n### ✅ Next sanity check\nRun this inside your poetry venv (`srcEnv`):\n\n```bash\npoetry run python -c \"import torch; print('CUDA:', torch.cuda.is_available()); print('Device:', torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\"\n```\n\nIf it prints:\n```\nCUDA: True\nDevice: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070\n```\nthen we’re golden.  \n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Making Zephyr actually use GPU\nIn your `run.py`, load the model like this:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\nimport torch\n\nmodel_id = \"HuggingFaceH4/zephyr-7b-beta\"\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id)\n\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    model_id,\n    torch_dtype=torch.float16,   # fp16 saves VRAM\n    device_map=\"auto\"            # auto = GPU if available\n)\n```\n\nThat combo will automatically stick Zephyr on your GPU.  \n\n---\n\n👉 Want me to edit your current `run.py` so it prints which device it’s running on at startup (CPU vs GPU) before you even type a prompt?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "give me the full file, please."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Got it 👍 — here’s a **self-contained `run.py`** you can drop in.  \nThis one:  \n\n- Uses **Zephyr-7B-Beta**  \n- Runs on **GPU if available**, otherwise falls back to CPU  \n- Prints out the **device it’s using** at startup  \n- Has a simple **chat loop** so you can type multiple prompts without restarting  \n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM, pipeline\nimport torch\n\ndef main():\n    model_id = \"HuggingFaceH4/zephyr-7b-beta\"\n\n    print(f\"Loading model: {model_id} ...\")\n\n    tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id)\n\n    # Load model with GPU if available\n    model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n        model_id,\n        torch_dtype=torch.float16 if torch.cuda.is_available() else torch.float32,\n        device_map=\"auto\"\n    )\n\n    device = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n    print(f\"✅ Model loaded on {device}\")\n\n    # Create a pipeline for text generation\n    pipe = pipeline(\n        \"text-generation\",\n        model=model,\n        tokenizer=tokenizer,\n        device=0 if torch.cuda.is_available() else -1\n    )\n\n    print(\"\\n=== Zephyr Chat ===\")\n    print(\"Type 'exit' to quit.\\n\")\n\n    while True:\n        prompt = input(\"You: \")\n        if prompt.lower() in {\"exit\", \"quit\"}:\n            print(\"👋 Goodbye!\")\n            break\n\n        outputs = pipe(\n            prompt,\n            max_new_tokens=200,\n            do_sample=True,\n            temperature=0.7,\n            top_p=0.9\n        )\n\n        response = outputs[0][\"generated_text\"][len(prompt):].strip()\n        print(f\"Zephyr: {response}\\n\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n---\n\n👉 Next step:  \nRun it with  \n\n```bash\npoetry run python run.py\n```\n\nand it’ll tell you whether Zephyr is running on **CPU or GPU**, then drop you into a little REPL chat.  \n\nWant me to also wire in **conversation memory** so it remembers the last few turns, or keep it strictly single-prompt/response for now?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
