om torch==2.5.1+cu121) (80.9.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: sympy==1.13.1 in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from torch==2.5.1+cu121) (1.13.1)\nRequirement already satisfied: numpy in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from torchvision==0.20.1+cu121) (2.2.6)\nRequirement already satisfied: pillow!=8.3.*,>=5.3.0 in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from torchvision==0.20.1+cu121) (11.3.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: nvidia-nvjitlink-cu12 in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from nvidia-cusolver-cu12==11.4.5.107->torch==2.5.1+cu121) (12.6.85)\nRequirement already satisfied: mpmath<1.4,>=1.1.0 in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from sympy==1.13.1->torch==2.5.1+cu121) (1.3.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: MarkupSafe>=2.0 in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from jinja2->torch==2.5.1+cu121) (3.0.2)\nInstalling collected packages: torchvision\nSuccessfully installed torchvision-0.20.1+cu121\ntorch: 2.5.1+cu121 CUDA: 12.1\nis_available: True\n/home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/cuda/__init__.py:235: UserWarning:\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU with CUDA capability sm_120 is not compatible with the current PyTorch installation.\nThe current PyTorch install supports CUDA capabilities sm_50 sm_60 sm_70 sm_75 sm_80 sm_86 sm_90.\nIf you want to use the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU GPU with PyTorch, please check the instructions at https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/\n\n  warnings.warn(\ncapability: (12, 0)\nname: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Ah—that output tells the whole story:\n\n- Your GPU reports **SM 12.0** (`capability: (12, 0)`), i.e. a **Blackwell-gen** RTX 5070 Laptop.\n- Your current wheel is **torch 2.5.1+cu121**, which only ships kernels up to **SM 90**.\n- Result: CUDA “available: True”, but when a CUDA kernel is actually needed you get *“no kernel image is available”* (exactly what you saw before).\n\nYou’ve got three good paths forward:\n\n---\n\n# Option A — Install a PyTorch build that supports SM_120 (easiest if available)\n\nTry the **nightly** wheels with a newer CUDA runtime. One of these indexes should contain builds that include **sm_120** (order matters; try top to bottom):\n\n```bash\n# Inside your poetry venv\npip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio\n\n# Try cu124 nightly\npip install --pre torch torchvision --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu124\n\n# If not found, try cu125 nightly\n# pip install --pre torch torchvision --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu125\n\n# If still no joy, try cu126 nightly\n# pip install --pre torch torchvision --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu126\n```\n\nVerify (no warning about “supports sm_50 … sm_90” should appear):\n\n```bash\npython - << 'PY'\nimport torch\nprint(torch.__version__, torch.version.cuda)\nprint(\"CUDA ok:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    print(\"capability:\", torch.cuda.get_device_capability(0))\nPY\n```\n\nIf the warning disappears and a quick `model.generate(...)` works, you’re done.\n\n---\n\n# Option B — Build PyTorch from source targeting SM_120 (reliable, but takes time)\n\nSince prebuilt 2.5.x wheels don’t include sm_120, compiling with **`TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"12.0\"`** ensures kernels for your card are emitted.\n\nMinimal, battle-tested config (no NCCL/NVML; GCC 12; CUDA 12.5+ preferred):\n\n```bash\n# Inside a clean checkout of pytorch (not the tarball). Use master or a recent 2.6/2.7 branch.\n# Prereqs\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential gcc-12 g++-12 cmake ninja-build python3-dev\n\n# Make sure nvcc is present and reasonably new (12.5+ ideal)\nnvcc --version\n\n# Use GCC-12 explicitly (nvcc dislikes GCC >12)\nexport CC=/usr/bin/gcc-12\nexport CXX=/usr/bin/g++-12\n\n# Only needed for *building* (wheels bundle what they need):\nexport TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"12.0\"\n\n# Keep the build small & avoid the NVML/NCCL trouble you hit earlier\nexport USE_CUDA=1\nexport USE_CUDNN=1\nexport USE_NCCL=0\nexport USE_NVML=0\nexport USE_DISTRIBUTED=0\nexport BUILD_TEST=0\n\n# (Optional) avoid third-party protobuf build headaches\nexport USE_SYSTEM_PROTOBUF=1\n\n# Install python deps (from repo root)\npip install -r requirements.txt\npip install setuptools==72.1.0  # matches what your env used successfully\n\n# Build & install\npython setup.py bdist_wheel\npip install dist/*.whl\n```\n\nRe-run the quick CUDA check. If it shows capability `(12, 0)` and no sm_90 warning, you’re good.\n\n---\n\n# Option C — Keep moving today (CPU or alternate backend)\n\n- **Force CPU** in your loader to keep developing prompts / plumbing while we fix GPU:\n  ```python\n  model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n      MODEL_ID,\n      torch_dtype=torch.float32,\n      device_map={\"\": \"cpu\"},\n  )\n  ```\n- Or use **llama.cpp** (it compiles CUDA kernels locally and can target sm_120); convert your model to GGUF and run with `LLAMA_CUDA=1`. That sidesteps PyTorch until proper wheels land.\n\n---\n\n## Two small cleanups in your code\n\n1) When you load with `device_map=\"auto\"`, **don’t** also pass `device=` to `pipeline()` (you saw that error once). Let Accelerate place the model.\n\n2) On Ada/Blackwell, prefer **bf16**:\n```python\ntorch_dtype = torch.bfloat16 if torch.cuda.is_available() else torch.float32\n```\n\n---\n\n### TL;DR\nYour card is **too new** for the cu121 wheels’ kernel set. Either grab a **nightly PyTorch** that includes **sm_120**, or **build from source** with `TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"12.0\"`. If you want, I can read your `pip install` output after trying cu124/cu125 nightly and tell you the next exact step."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "4.whl.metadata (1.5 kB)\nCollecting pytorch-triton==3.2.0+git4b3bb1f8 (from torch)\n  Downloading https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/pytorch_triton-3.2.0%2Bgit4b3bb1f8-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_27_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl.metadata (1.3 kB)\nRequirement already satisfied: numpy in /home/todd/srcEnv/lib/python3.12/site-packages (from torchvision) (2.2.6)\nINFO: pip is looking at multiple versions of torchvision to determine which version is compatible with other requirements. This could take a while.\nERROR: Cannot install torch and torchvision==0.22.0.dev20250226+cu124 because these package versions have conflicting dependencies.\n\nThe conflict is caused by:\n    The user requested torch\n    torchvision 0.22.0.dev20250226+cu124 depends on torch==2.7.0.dev20250226\n\nTo fix this you could try to:\n1. loosen the range of package versions you've specified\n2. remove package versions to allow pip attempt to solve the dependency conflict\n\nERROR: ResolutionImpossible: for help visit https://pip.pypa.io/en/latest/topics/dependency-resolution/#dealing-with-dependency-conflicts"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You hit a classic nightly mismatch: your **torch** wheel and **torchvision** wheel must be the **same build date** (and same CUDA tag). Your log shows:\n\n- `torchvision==0.22.0.dev20250226+cu124` → **requires** `torch==2.7.0.dev20250226`\n- You installed a different torch nightly, so pip balked.\n\nYou’ve got three clean fixes—pick one:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix A (pin both to the same date)\n```bash\n# in your poetry venv\npip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio pytorch-triton\n\n# install matching date + CUDA tag (note the same 20250226 + cu124)\npip install --pre --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu124 \\\n  \"torch==2.7.0.dev20250226+cu124\" \\\n  \"torchvision==0.22.0.dev20250226+cu124\"\n```\n(If pip says that exact torch build isn’t found, list available builds by trying `pip install --pre torch==2.7.0.dev2025* --index-url ...` and pick the date that **also** exists for torchvision, then install both with that same date.)\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix B (don’t install torchvision)\nYou’re doing text LLMs—**you don’t need torchvision**.\n```bash\npip uninstall -y torchvision torchaudio pytorch-triton\npip install --pre --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu124 torch\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix C (auto-match torchvision to whatever torch you installed)\nInstall torch first, then a matching torchvision without pin; pip will pick the same-date build:\n```bash\npip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio pytorch-triton\npip install --pre --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu124 torch\npip install --pre --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu124 torchvision\n```\n\n---\n\n### After install — quick sanity check\n```bash\npython - << 'PY'\nimport torch\nprint(\"torch:\", torch.__version__, \"CUDA:\", torch.version.cuda)\nprint(\"CUDA available:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    print(\"capability:\", torch.cuda.get_device_capability(0))\n    print(\"name:\", torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\nPY\n```\n- You should **not** see the old warning about “supports sm_50 … sm_90”.\n- Capability should show `(12, 0)` and generation should run without “no kernel image” errors.\n\nIf the **sm_120** warning persists with cu124 nightly, repeat with cu125 (or cu126) nightlies, same steps but swap the index URL to `.../cu125` (and use matching date pins)."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "the thing we need to do is ensure that poetry doesn't fricking *downgrade* working environments. How do we do that?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Short answer: make the **lockfile the law**, and pin any “touchy” packages (like torch) to exact builds from explicit indexes. Then never run a full `poetry update` unless you mean it.\n\nHere’s the playbook that stops surprise downgrades:\n\n---\n\n### 1) Treat `poetry.lock` as the source of truth\n- Check it into git.\n- On machines (or after nuking a venv) use:\n  ```bash\n  poetry install --sync\n  ```\n  `--sync` makes the venv **exactly** match the lock (adding/removing to fit it). No upgrades, no downgrades, no resolver games.\n\n**Golden rule:** Don’t run `poetry update` unless you intend to re-resolve everything.\n\n---\n\n### 2) Pin “sensitive” deps exactly & from explicit sources\nPoetry will downgrade if a dependency graph forces it. Avoid that by **pinning** CUDA builds and telling Poetry exactly where to get them.\n\nIn `pyproject.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[tool.poetry]\nname = \"oss20b_test\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\npackage-mode = false\ndescription = \"\"\nauthors = [\"Todd\"]\n\n[tool.poetry.dependencies]\npython = \">=3.12,<3.13\"\n\n# Pin exact CUDA wheels (example: cu121 stable)\ntorch = { version = \"==2.5.1+cu121\", source = \"pytorch-cu121\" }\ntorchvision = { version = \"==0.20.1+cu121\", source = \"pytorch-cu121\" }\n\ntransformers = \"^4.44.2\"\naccelerate = \"^0.33.0\"\ndatasets = \"^4.0.0\"\nsentencepiece = \"^0.2.0\"\nnumpy = \">=2.2,<3.0\"\n# …pin other “hot” libs that caused churn\n\n[tool.poetry.group.dev.dependencies]\nipython = \"^8.26.0\"\nruff = \"^0.5.7\"\n\n# Repos: make the CUDA index explicit so Poetry doesn’t mix sources.\n[tool.poetry.source]\nname = \"pypi\"\nurl = \"https://pypi.org/simple\"\npriority = \"primary\"\n\n[[tool.poetry.source]]\nname = \"pytorch-cu121\"\nurl = \"https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121\"\npriority = \"explicit\"   # only used if a dep explicitly opts into this source\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `priority = \"explicit\"` prevents Poetry from “helpfully” pulling random versions from that index unless you ask (via the `source` field on the dependency).\n- If you move to nightlies, make a `pytorch-cu124-nightly` source and pin both `torch` and `torchvision` to the **same date** build.\n\n---\n\n### 3) Day-to-day commands (safe)\n- Install exactly what’s in the lock:\n  ```bash\n  poetry install --sync\n  ```\n- Add a package **without** disturbing others:\n  - First, **pin** it (e.g., `somepkg = \"==1.2.3\"` in `pyproject.toml`) then:\n    ```bash\n    poetry update somepkg\n    poetry install --sync\n    ```\n  This updates only that package (and whatever it strictly needs), keeping the rest stable.\n- Update a single pinned dep:\n  ```bash\n  poetry add torch==2.7.0+cu124 --source pytorch-cu124\n  # or\n  poetry update torch torchvision\n  ```\n  Avoid plain `poetry update` (updates *everything* → common cause of downgrades).\n\n---\n\n### 4) Use groups to isolate GPU stack\nKeep your fragile GPU stack separate so adding unrelated libs doesn’t poke it:\n\n```toml\n[tool.poetry.group.gpu.dependencies]\ntorch = { version = \"==2.5.1+cu121\", source = \"pytorch-cu121\" }\ntorchvision = { version = \"==0.20.1+cu121\", source = \"pytorch-cu121\" }\n```\n\nInstall with:\n```bash\npoetry install --with gpu --sync\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5) When you must experiment, don’t risk the main env\nTwo good patterns:\n- **Branch + lock**: work on a git branch, run `poetry update` there, and only merge if you’re happy.\n- **Second venv**: `poetry env use 3.12 && poetry install --sync` for stable; clone the project to a sibling dir for experiments.\n\n---\n\n### 6) If Poetry *still* wants to downgrade\n- There’s a conflicting dep. Run:\n  ```bash\n  poetry update -v\n  ```\n  and read the resolver’s “because … requires …” lines. Pin the offender (or relax the constraint you actually want relaxed) and re-lock only the minimal set:\n  ```bash\n  poetry update offendingpkg otheroffender\n  poetry install --sync\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### 7) Last resort: generate an immutable pip constraints file\nIf you need “zero surprises” CI:\n```bash\npoetry export -f requirements.txt --without-hashes -o requirements.lock.txt\npip install -r requirements.lock.txt\n```\n(You can still use Poetry locally; CI uses the exported, fully pinned list.)\n\n---\n\nStick to that workflow and Poetry won’t downgrade a working GPU stack again. If you want, paste your current `pyproject.toml` and I’ll pin the exact entries and sources for your chosen CUDA/Nightly combo."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
