{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Can we use \n\n    Noctua NF-F12 PWM, Premium Quiet Fan, 4-Pin (120mm, Brown)Noctua NF-F12 PWM with the Fractal North case?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes—you can **use the Noctua NF-F12 PWM 120mm fan** in the **Fractal Design North** case, but there are some important limitations and considerations to keep in mind:\n\n---\n\n###  Compatibility Overview\n\n####  Supported Fan Mounts\n- The North mid-tower case supports up to **six 120mm fans** or **four 140mm fans** in total citeturn0search10turn0search6.\n- The top panel has two 120mm fan slots:\n  - One for exhaust.\n  - One for intake (depending on your setup) citeturn0search10turn0search6.\n- Rear supports a single **120mm exhaust** fan—**not 140mm** citeturn0search1turn0search10.\n\nSo your NF-F12 120mm fan is perfectly compatible in the top and rear positions, but a 140mm variant wouldn’t fit the rear location.\n\n####  Fan Hub and PWM Control\n- The **mesh version** of the North includes a **PWM fan hub**, which lets you control multiple fans via a single 4-pin motherboard header citeturn0search8turn0search7.\n- That hub feeds the signal to all connected fans—so if you install an NF-F12 PWM on the hub, it will respond to the same controller as the stock fans citeturn0search7turn0search8.\n- Just ensure the motherboard header you use can supply enough current if you connect too many fans together citeturn0search7turn0search8.\n\n---\n\n###  Summary\n\n-  **Yes**, NF-F12 PWM fans are supported in all 120mm-compatible fan slots (top and rear).\n-  **No**, you cannot use a 140mm fan in the rear slot.\n-  **Yes**, if you’re using the mesh version, the included PWM hub will let you control NF-F12 fans alongside stock ones—just be mindful of power limits.\n\n---\n\n###  Bottom Line\nGo ahead and install the NF-F12 PWM fans—you’ll enjoy excellent static pressure, quiet operation, and full compatibility with the North case (in 120mm slots). Let me know if you’d like help planning an optimal fan layout or picking placement!\n\nWant suggestions for front or side intake setups using NF-F12s?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "where do i learn more about AI development"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great question — there are lots of excellent places to dive into AI development depending on how you like to learn and your current skill level.\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 **Beginner-Friendly Learning Paths**\n\n**📘 Online Courses**\n- **Coursera** – Andrew Ng’s *Machine Learning* and *Deep Learning Specialization* are classics.\n- **edX** – MIT’s *Introduction to AI* course is very hands-on.\n- **Udacity** – *AI Programming with Python Nanodegree* is a structured way to start coding AI.\n\n**📚 Interactive Platforms**\n- **freeCodeCamp** – Entirely free, with guided projects in machine learning.\n- **Kaggle Learn** – Bite-sized tutorials where you can code in the browser.\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ **Hands-On Learning**\n\n**🔬 Practice Projects**\n- Compete in Kaggle competitions.\n- Build simple projects like spam classifiers, chatbots, or image recognizers using Python libraries like TensorFlow, PyTorch, or scikit-learn.\n\n**💡 Open-Source Repos to Explore**\n- Google’s **TensorFlow tutorials**\n- PyTorch’s **Learning-to-learn examples**\n- OpenAI’s **gym** for reinforcement learning environments\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Books Worth Reading\n- *Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow* by Aurélien Géron\n- *Deep Learning* by Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville (more advanced)\n- *AI: A Modern Approach* by Russell & Norvig (foundational theory)\n\n---\n\n### 🌍 Community & Inspiration\n\n- Follow AI news and papers on **arXiv**, **Hugging Face**, **Reddit r/MachineLearning**\n- Join communities like **Discord servers**, **Meetups**, or **Google Developer Groups** for AI\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to put together a customized roadmap based on your current skill level (e.g., beginner, intermediate, advanced) and learning style?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "how do i get to open AI"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "iturn0image4\n\nHere’s a view of what the entrance to OpenAI's former San Francisco office looks like, though today the company operates from different locations.citeturn0image4\n\n---\n\n##  Where Is OpenAI Located?\n\nOpenAI’s **current primary headquarters** is located at:\n\n**3180 18th Street, Suite 100, San Francisco, CA 94110, United States** citeturn0search3turn0search2turn0search11turn0search14.\n\nThis facility serves as the main hub for OpenAI’s research, engineering, product development, operations, and leadership.citeturn0search11\n\nPrevious to this, OpenAI operated in the historic **Pioneer Building** (also on 18th Street in the Mission District). However, they moved out in August 2024.citeturn0search22turn0search12\n\n---\n\n##  How Can You *Get* to OpenAI’s HQ?\n\nIf you’re planning a visit to **3180 18th Street** in San Francisco, here’s what to keep in mind:\n\n### 1. **Transportation Options**\n- **By public transit**: Depending on where you're coming from, you can take BART or Muni into downtown SF, then hop on a bus or light rail that stops near 18th Street and Folsom, or walk from nearby stations like *Civic Center* or *16th Street Mission*.\n- **By car or ride-share**: You can drive or take a ride-share using the exact address; note that parking can be scarce in the Mission District.\n\n### 2. **Building Access**\n- The HQ is secure: entry typically requires a **keycard or being buzzed in**. Journalists and casual visitors have been turned away by security unless they have scheduled appointments.citeturn0search12turn0search7\n- So if you're hoping to visit, make sure to **arrange an appointment in advance** with your contact at OpenAI.\n\n### 3. **Context**\n- In recent years, OpenAI has significantly expanded its office footprint in the **Mission Bay** area — including major leases at 550 Terry A. Francois Blvd., 1455 and 1515 Third Street (subleased from Uber), and potentially new space at 455 Mission Bay Blvd.citeturn0news20\n- These expansions suggest they’re establishing a multi-building campus in that neighborhood.\n\n---\n\n##  Quick Summary\n\n| Question                   | Details                                                                 |\n|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Headquarters Address**   | 3180 18th Street, Suite 100, San Francisco, CA 94110                    |\n| **Old HQ Location**        | Pioneer Building on 18th Street (vacated in 2024)                       |\n| **Access Requirements**    | Secure entrance — keycard or appointment needed                         |\n| **Nearby Expansions**      | Mission Bay locations: Terry Francois Blvd., Third Street, etc.         |\n\n---\n\n##  What Would You Like to Do?\n\n- Are you planning a **visit** and need help with scheduling or navigating public transit routes?\n- Or are you interested just in the **history of OpenAI’s office evolution** in San Francisco?\n\nLet me know!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Got a nice curry recipe with green lentils?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Sure — here’s a hearty, flavourful green lentil curry that’s easy to make and great for batch cooking or freezing.\n\n---\n\n## **Green Lentil Curry (serves 4–6)**\n\n### **Ingredients**\n- 1 tbsp oil (olive, coconut or ghee)\n- 1 large onion, finely chopped  \n- 3–4 cloves garlic, minced  \n- 1 thumb-sized piece fresh ginger, grated  \n- 1–2 green chillies, finely sliced (optional)\n- 2 tsp ground cumin  \n- 2 tsp ground coriander  \n- 1 tsp turmeric  \n- 1 tsp garam masala  \n- ½ tsp chilli powder (adjust to taste)\n- 250 g *green lentils* (dry), rinsed well  \n- 400 g tin chopped tomatoes (or passata)  \n- 400 ml coconut milk (full-fat or light)  \n- 500 ml vegetable stock  \n- Salt & pepper, to taste  \n- Juice of ½ lime or lemon  \n- Fresh coriander (cilantro), for garnish  \n\n### **Method**\n1. **Prep aromatics:**  \n   Heat the oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Add onions and cook until soft and golden (6–8 min). Stir in garlic, ginger, and green chilli, cook 1 min more.\n\n2. **Add spices:**  \n   Add cumin, coriander, turmeric, garam masala, and chilli powder. Stir for 30 seconds to bloom the spices.\n\n3. **Lentils & liquids:**  \n   Add green lentils and stir to coat. Add chopped tomatoes, coconut milk, and stock. Mix well and bring to a boil.\n\n4. **Simmer:**  \n   Reduce heat, cover loosely, and simmer for 30–35 minutes, stirring occasionally, until lentils are tender and sauce has thickened. Add more stock/water if needed.\n\n5. **Finish:**  \n   Season with salt, pepper, and lime/lemon juice. Taste and adjust spices.\n\n6. **Serve:**  \n   Garnish with fresh coriander. Perfect with rice, naan, or as a hearty soup.\n\n---\n\nWant a variation (e.g., spinach version, slow cooker instructions, or using tinned lentils)?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
