{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "yes, please! Let's get that!"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here’s a quick Python script to verify your **CUDA installation** and **GPU availability** with PyTorch:\n\n---\n\n## 🧪 `cuda_check.py`\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"Checking CUDA support via PyTorch...\\n\")\n\n    cuda_available = torch.cuda.is_available()\n    print(f\"✅ CUDA Available: {cuda_available}\")\n\n    if cuda_available:\n        print(f\"🖥️ GPU Name: {torch.cuda.get_device_name(0)}\")\n        print(f\"🔢 Device Count: {torch.cuda.device_count()}\")\n        print(f\"📦 Current Device: cuda:{torch.cuda.current_device()}\")\n        print(f\"💾 Memory Allocated: {torch.cuda.memory_allocated() / 1024**2:.2f} MB\")\n        print(f\"💾 Memory Reserved:  {torch.cuda.memory_reserved() / 1024**2:.2f} MB\")\n    else:\n        print(\"⚠️  CUDA not available. Check drivers, installation, and PyTorch build.\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 How to run:\n\n```bash\npython cuda_check.py\n```\n\nIf CUDA is properly installed and your GPU is available, you’ll see your GPU name and status.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Note:\nThis script depends on PyTorch. If it’s not installed yet:\n\n```bash\npip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121\n```\n\n(Replace `cu121` with your CUDA version if different.)\n\nLet me know if you'd like a **TensorFlow version** or a **system-wide NVIDIA diagnostics script** too."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
