{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "when I do a google search, it used to return stats on how many pages were found, can we do something like this again? I want to evaluate potential customers and one way is simply to see how much they're referenced on google."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, Google used to show a total number of search results at the top (\"About 1,230,000 results in 0.32 seconds\"), but they've **largely removed** or **obscured** this feature in recent years — especially in newer interfaces (mobile, logged-in sessions, EU privacy zones, etc.).\n\nThat said, you **can still estimate visibility in several ways**, even programmatically. Here are your options:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Option 1: Use Google Search with Parameters (Manually)**\n\n- Format:\n  ```\n  site:example.com OR \"Person Name\" OR \"Company Name\"\n  ```\n\n- Add `&num=100` to the URL for more results per page\n- You can still **visually estimate** by scrolling multiple pages or using scraping tools\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 **Option 2: Programmatic Search with SerpAPI** (or similar)\n\nGoogle itself doesn't offer a public API for full search result counts anymore, but **SerpAPI** does.\n\n```python\nfrom serpapi import GoogleSearch\n\nparams = {\n    \"q\": \"Todd McCaffrey\",\n    \"api_key\": \"YOUR_SERPAPI_KEY\"\n}\n\nsearch = GoogleSearch(params)\nresults = search.get_dict()\nprint(results.get(\"search_information\", {}).get(\"total_results\"))\n```\n\n- You’ll get accurate `total_results`\n- ⚠ Requires SerpAPI key (free tier available)\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 **Option 3: Use Bing Search API (Microsoft Azure)**\n\nBing’s API **still returns result estimates**:\n```python\nimport requests\n\nheaders = {\"Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key\": \"YOUR_BING_KEY\"}\nparams = {\"q\": \"Todd McCaffrey\"}\nr = requests.get(\"https://api.bing.microsoft.com/v7.0/search\", headers=headers, params=params)\ndata = r.json()\nprint(data[\"webPages\"][\"totalEstimatedMatches\"])\n```\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Ideal For Your Use Case?\n\nIf you're **evaluating clients for toddbot or University of Souls**, using something like SerpAPI or Bing Search API lets you:\n\n- Score their **digital footprint** (Google/Bing mentions)\n- Combine with social media or LinkedIn data\n- Automate the intake evaluation pipeline\n\n---\n\nWant a script or Streamlit app to do this for a list of names?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Let's start with a script, please."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "It looks like the script failed due to a **DNS resolution issue** or lack of internet access in this environment. However, I can give you a clean **ready-to-use Python script** you can run locally to measure \"web presence\" (search result counts) using the **Bing Web Search API**:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ `google_footprint.py` (actually uses Bing due to Google's restrictions)\n\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport requests\n\ndef bing_search_count(query: str, api_key: str) -> int:\n    url = \"https://api.bing.microsoft.com/v7.0/search\"\n    headers = {\"Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key\": api_key}\n    params = {\"q\": query}\n    \n    response = requests.get(url, headers=headers, params=params)\n    response.raise_for_status()\n    data = response.json()\n    \n    return data.get(\"webPages\", {}).get(\"totalEstimatedMatches\", 0)\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Estimate Google/Bing web footprint for a given name or brand\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"query\", help=\"Name, phrase, or company to search for\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--api-key\", required=True, help=\"Your Bing Search v7 API key\")\n\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    \n    try:\n        count = bing_search_count(args.query, args.api_key)\n        print(f\"\\n🔍 '{args.query}' → Estimated matches: {count:,}\")\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"❌ Error: {e}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ How to Use\n\n1. Get a free Bing Search v7 API key at:  \n   [https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/bing/apis/bing-search-api-v7](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/bing/apis/bing-search-api-v7)\n\n2. Run it:\n\n```bash\npython google_footprint.py \"Todd McCaffrey\" --api-key YOUR_BING_KEY\n```\n\n---\n\nWould you like this wrapped into a bulk-evaluator (i.e. multiple names from a CSV or JSON)?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "okay, we finished with\nsudo apt install -y nvidia-cuda-toolkit\nnow what (for our LLM training)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great! Now that you've successfully installed the `nvidia-cuda-toolkit`, and assuming your GPU (RTX 4070) is correctly configured and visible to the system (`nvidia-smi` confirms that), you're ready to begin **fine-tuning or training your LLM (Mistral-7B)** using LoRA.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Recap: Your Setup\n\n- ✅ CUDA installed (`nvidia-smi` and `nvcc --version` should now both work)\n- ✅ Mistral-7B selected\n- ✅ LoRA pipeline created in `/home/todd/linux/Projects/todd/Con00/mistral_lora_project`\n- ✅ Training data in `toddmccaffrey_clean_qa.jsonl` (and evaluation set)\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Next Steps: Fine-Tune the LLM (Mistral-7B) with LoRA\n\n### 1. **Check everything is CUDA-aware**\nMake sure `torch` sees the GPU:\n\n```python\npython -c \"import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available()); print(torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))\"\n```\n\nShould return:\n```text\nTrue\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Launch LoRA Fine-Tuning**\nAssuming you're in your `mistral_lora_project` directory:\n\n```bash\ncd /home/todd/linux/Projects/todd/Con00/mistral_lora_project\npython train.py\n```\n\nThis will:\n\n- Load Mistral-7B in 4-bit quantized mode\n- Apply LoRA adapters on attention layers\n- Fine-tune on your custom Q&A JSONL dataset\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Monitor GPU Usage (Optional)**\nIn a second terminal tab:\n\n```bash\nwatch -n 2 nvidia-smi\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Evaluate / Use Trained Model**\nAfter training, your merged model will be in `./final_model/`. You can run it via:\n\n```bash\npython chat.py\n```\n\nOr use `transformers` to load it directly for inference:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"./final_model\", device_map=\"auto\")\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"./final_model\")\n\ninput_ids = tokenizer(\"Who is Todd McCaffrey?\", return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()\noutputs = model.generate(input_ids, max_new_tokens=100)\nprint(tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True))\n```\n\n---\n\nWould you like a dashboard to monitor training? Or a faster LoRA method like `qlora`?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
