{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "All I can do is make lots of files and folders. I can't find an import anywhere. Help!"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here’s how to *import* your `.md` file into StackEdit in the cloud — definitely *not* via a simple disk \"Import\" button, but rather through its sync functionality:\n\n---\n\n## ☁️ Step-by-Step Import via Google Drive or Dropbox\n\n1. **Go to StackEdit.io** and click **Start writing**.\n2. In the **top-left**, click the **folder icon** to open the Documents panel citeturn0search9turn0search11.\n3. Still in that left panel, locate the **“Import/Export”** or **synchronization** options citeturn0search11.\n4. Choose **“Import from Google Drive”** or **“Import from Dropbox”** — you’ll need to authorize StackEdit if you haven’t already citeturn0search4turn0search11.\n5. Select your uploaded `.md` file from the cloud storage — StackEdit will download and open it locally in your browser.\n6. Once open, your file is editable, with real-time preview & auto-save in browser storage citeturn0search3turn0search11.\n7. **To save changes back** to Drive/Dropbox, click the **sync** icon (circular arrow) at the top citeturn0search3turn0search11.\n\n---\n\n## ✈️ Tips for Flight Mode Editing:\n- StackEdit **auto-saves locally**, so editing offline is safe citeturn0search3turn0search4.\n- When you're back online, hit *Sync* to push your edits up.\n- Want to stick to one source? Keep your file only in Google Drive or Dropbox — then import into StackEdit and edit live.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Quick Summary\n\n| Task                   | Steps                                                                 |\n|------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Import `.md` file      | Use Drive/Dropbox sync, not direct disk import                        |\n| Edit offline           | Supported — local auto-save works during flight                  