{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "so, in your md, you have \"cue\" -- what do you mean by that?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great catch — “**cue**” is a term I used to help seed the emotional or sensory **detail** that brings the memory to life.\n\n### 🎯 In this context, a “cue” is:\n> A **specific sensory**, **emotional**, or **narrative fragment** that helps the LLM (or a human editor) recreate the feeling or *vibe* of the memory.\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Why include it?\n- It guides **voice tone** and **personality shaping** (e.g. calm vs chaotic)\n- Helps the LLM **hallucinate less** by anchoring to texture/sensation\n- Makes future recall feel vivid and natural (good for toddlerBot or future Todd)\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Examples of cues:\n\n| Event                  | Cue                              | Why It Matters                        |\n|------------------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| First bath             | Warm water, mother’s hum         | Evokes security, sensory memory        |\n| Saw a dog bark         | Loud, jumpy sound, surprised face| Helps model early emotional response   |\n| Ate lemon              | Sour taste, wrinkled nose        | Anchors sensory + behavioral reaction  |\n| Picked up keys         | Cool metal, jangling sound       | Tactile + auditory for memory realism  |\n\n---\n\nSo the **Detail / Cue** column is really a way to say:\n\n> _“What did it feel/smell/look/sound like? What stands out?”_\n\nIt gives the LLM (and the human) a foothold in memory construction.\n\nWant me to rename the column from “Detail / Cue” to something more human like “Memory Texture” or “Sensory Detail”?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "For example you have 1961-06 -- what does the -06 mean and do you have a legend?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Excellent question — and you're right again to call out that **\"1961-06\"** might not be self-evident without a legend or clear convention.\n\n---\n\n## 📆 What does `1961-06` mean?\n\nIt follows the **ISO 8601 date format**, which means:\n\n> `YYYY-MM` = **Year and Month**\n\nSo:\n\n- `1961-06` = **June 1961**\n- `1962-03` = **March 1962**\n- `1963-02` = **February 1963**\n\nWe’re using this to suggest **approximate timing**, not exact dates — since memories from age 0–2 are often reconstructed, vague, or symbolic.\n\n---\n\n## 📘 Legend / Conventions You Should Probably Add\n\nTo make your timeline self-documenting, you can add a “Legend” or “Conventions” block either in the YAML header or just after the table.\n\nHere’s a suggested version:\n\n```markdown\n<!--\n🗂️ Conventions:\n- Date Format: YYYY-MM (e.g., 1961-06 = June 1961)\n- Age Format: decimal years (e.g., 0.2y = 2–3 months)\n- Type: Milestone, Emotion, Language, Memory, Sensory, etc.\n- Source: Imagined, Reconstructed, Family Story, Historical\n- Tags: comma-separated themes (e.g., motor, bonding, fear)\n\nThis timeline is approximate and may include symbolic or poetic memories.\n-->\n```\n\nWould you like me to update the Markdown file with this convention block for clarity?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
