/Con00/nemo_env/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/nn/modules/module.py\", line 900, in _apply\n    module._apply(fn)\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/Projects/todd/Con00/nemo_env/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/nn/modules/module.py\", line 900, in _apply\n    module._apply(fn)\n  [Previous line repeated 5 more times]\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/Projects/todd/Con00/nemo_env/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/nn/modules/module.py\", line 927, in _apply\n    param_applied = fn(param)\n                    ^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/Projects/todd/Con00/nemo_env/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/nn/modules/module.py\", line 1333, in convert\n    raise NotImplementedError(\nNotImplementedError: Cannot copy out of meta tensor; no data! Please use torch.nn.Module.to_empty() instead of torch.nn.Module.to() when moving module from meta to a different device."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Absolutely! Below is a **template for a HuggingFace Transformers + LoRA fine-tuning script** (`train_lora.py`) that incorporates all best practices for your scenario, with:\n\n- **Correct model loading** (avoids meta tensor errors)\n- **Padding token fix**\n- **Flexible mapping for dataset fields**\n- **LoRA integration (with PEFT)**\n- **Trainer setup**\n\nYou’ll need to adapt paths/model names and LoRA config as appropriate for your project.  \n**If you want something even more custom (custom data fields, advanced LoRA), just tell me your needs!**\n\n---\n\n## **train_lora.py**\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import (\n    AutoTokenizer,\n    AutoModelForCausalLM,\n    Trainer,\n    TrainingArguments,\n)\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model\n\ndef train():\n    # === CONFIGURATION ===\n    model_name = \"mistralai/Mistral-7B-v0.1\"  # Change as needed\n    data_path = \"/home/todd/Projects/todd/Con00/mistral_lora_project/data/train.jsonl\"  # Change as needed\n\n    # === LOAD DATA ===\n    dataset = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=data_path, split=\"train\")\n    print(\"Sample record:\", dataset[0])\n\n    # === LOAD TOKENIZER ===\n    tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name, use_fast=True)\n    # Make sure padding token exists\n    if tokenizer.pad_token is None:\n        tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n\n    # === TOKENIZE DATA ===\n    # Assume your .jsonl has fields \"prompt\" and \"completion\" (customize as needed!)\n    def tokenize_fn(batch):\n        # Join prompt + completion, or just prompt as needed\n        text = batch.get(\"prompt\", \"\") + \" \" + batch.get(\"completion\", \"\")\n        return tokenizer(\n            text,\n            truncation=True,\n            padding=\"max_length\",\n            max_length=512,\n        )\n    tokenized_dataset = dataset.map(tokenize_fn, batched=False)\n    print(\"Sample tokenized:\", tokenized_dataset[0])\n\n    # === LOAD MODEL (NO META TENSORS) ===\n    model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n        model_name,\n        low_cpu_mem_usage=False,       # <-- This avoids meta tensor errors!\n        torch_dtype=torch.float16 if torch.cuda.is_available() else torch.float32,\n        device_map=None                # Loads on CPU; Trainer will move to GPU\n    )\n\n    # === INTEGRATE LORA (PEFT) ===\n    lora_config = LoraConfig(\n        r=8,\n        lora_alpha=32,\n        target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"v_proj\"],  # Example; adjust for your model\n        lora_dropout=0.05,\n        bias=\"none\",\n        task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n    )\n    model = get_peft_model(model, lora_config)\n\n    # === TRAINING ARGUMENTS ===\n    training_args = TrainingArguments(\n        per_device_train_batch_size=2,     # Adjust to your VRAM\n        num_train_epochs=3,\n        learning_rate=2e-4,\n        fp16=torch.cuda.is_available(),\n        output_dir=\"./lora_out\",\n        logging_steps=10,\n        save_steps=100,\n        save_total_limit=2,\n        remove_unused_columns=False,\n        report_to=\"none\",\n    )\n\n    # === TRAINER ===\n    trainer = Trainer(\n        model=model,\n        args=training_args,\n        train_dataset=tokenized_dataset,\n        tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    )\n\n    trainer.train()\n    print(\"Training finished!\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    train()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **How to Use:**\n\n1. **Check your `train.jsonl` data structure.**  \n   Example line:\n   ```json\n   {\"prompt\": \"What is the capital of France?\", \"completion\": \"Paris.\"}\n   ```\n   Adjust the `tokenize_fn` if your fields are different.\n\n2. **Install required packages:**\n   ```bash\n   pip install transformers datasets peft\n   ```\n\n3. **Run your script:**\n   ```bash\n   python train_lora.py\n   ```\n\n4. **Results and checkpoints will go into `./lora_out`.**\n\n---\n\n### **Notes:**\n- **If you want to use only the prompt field:**  \n  Change\n  ```python\n  text = batch.get(\"prompt\", \"\") + \" \" + batch.get(\"completion\", \"\")\n  ```\n  to just\n  ```python\n  text = batch.get(\"prompt\", \"\")\n  ```\n- **Adjust `target_modules` in `LoraConfig` as needed for your model architecture.**\n\n---\n\n**Let me know if you have a different data structure, want evaluation/validation split, or need custom logging!**  \nYou’re super close—just plug in your specifics and this will work."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "can you give me the full file?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "**Great job—this is a classic HuggingFace Trainer gotcha!**\n\nThe error:\n```\nValueError: Unable to create tensor ... your features (`prompt` in this case) have excessive nesting (inputs type `list` where type `int` is expected).\n```\nmeans:  \n**Your dataset still has the original 'prompt' and 'completion' string fields, but Trainer expects batches of `input_ids` and `attention_mask` (tensors, not strings).**\n\n---\n\n## **How to Fix: Only keep model-ready features!**\n\n### **Solution**\nAfter tokenization, **remove** the original string fields (`prompt`, `completion`) so the Trainer only sees the expected tokenized fields.\n\n---\n\n### **Update your code like this:**\n\n```python\ndef tokenize_fn(batch):\n    text = batch.get(\"prompt\", \"\") + \" \" + batch.get(\"completion\", \"\")\n    out = tokenizer(\n        text,\n        truncation=True,\n        padding=\"max_length\",\n        max_length=512,\n    )\n    # Remove raw string fields!\n    return {\n        \"input_ids\": out[\"input_ids\"],\n        \"attention_mask\": out[\"attention_mask\"],\n        # If you want labels to be the same as input_ids (typical for LM fine-tuning):\n        \"labels\": out[\"input_ids\"]\n    }\n\ntokenized_dataset = dataset.map(tokenize_fn, batched=False, remove_columns=dataset.column_names)\n```\n\n- The **important part** is `remove_columns=dataset.column_names` in your `map()` call.  \n- This strips out all fields except what you return (`input_ids`, `attention_mask`, and optionally `labels`).\n\n---\n\n### **Why does this work?**\n- Trainer **only wants to see model-ready features**: `input_ids`, `attention_mask`, and (optionally) `labels`.\n- If extra string fields remain, batching/collation fails.\n\n---\n\n### **Full Example Snippet:**\n\n```python\ndef tokenize_fn(batch):\n    text = batch.get(\"prompt\", \"\") + \" \" + batch.get(\"completion\", \"\")\n    out = tokenizer(\n        text,\n        truncation=True,\n        padding=\"max_length\",\n        max_length=512,\n    )\n    return {\n        \"input_ids\": out[\"input_ids\"],\n        \"attention_mask\": out[\"attention_mask\"],\n        \"labels\": out[\"input_ids\"]\n    }\n\ntokenized_dataset = dataset.map(\n    tokenize_fn,\n    batched=False,\n    remove_columns=dataset.column_names\n)\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Summary of fix:**\n- **Remove all non-tokenized fields** after mapping.\n- **Always provide `labels`** for causal LM fine-tuning (unless using special loss functions).\n\n---\n\n**Try this and your Trainer should run!**  \nLet me know if you want a full revised script or hit another error."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
