^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nweb-1  |   File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/gunicorn/workers/base.py\", line 204, in handle_abort\nweb-1  |     sys.exit(1)\nweb-1  | SystemExit: 1\nweb-1  | [2025-06-21 23:13:52 +0000] [16] [INFO] Worker exiting (pid: 16)\nweb-1  | [2025-06-21 23:13:52 +0000] [17] [INFO] Booting worker with pid: 17\n(hubEnv) todd@mona:~/linux/src/surveyhub$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "**This Gunicorn error** is still the same “worker timeout”/“no URI read” you saw earlier,  \nand **does not contain the real Python traceback from your Django app**.  \nThis means **Gunicorn is unable to serve requests because your app is failing to respond**, but it’s swallowing the real error.\n\n---\n\n## **What to do next (to get the *real* error):**\n\n1. **Temporarily stop Gunicorn in your Docker container.**  \n   (Either by changing the CMD in your `Dockerfile` to run the Django dev server,  \n   or by running the dev server manually inside the container after `docker compose exec web bash`.)\n\n2. **Set `DEBUG = True` in your prod settings for just this test.**  \n   (Don’t forget to set it back after!)\n\n3. **Run the dev server inside the container:**\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000 --settings=surveyhub.settings.prod\n   ```\n   Then visit `http://localhost:8000` and **you will see the real Python error page in your browser**.\n\n---\n\n### **Why do this?**\n- **Gunicorn** often swallows the true error, especially when it occurs on startup or outside of the request cycle.\n- The Django dev server will always show you a detailed Python traceback in your browser (when `DEBUG=True`).\n- Once you see the actual Python exception and traceback, you can fix the root cause.\n\n---\n\n## **What to do: Step by step**\n\n1. In your container or Dockerfile, set `CMD` to:\n    ```dockerfile\n    CMD [\"python\", \"manage.py\", \"runserver\", \"0.0.0.0:8000\", \"--settings=surveyhub.settings.prod\"]\n    ```\n2. Or just exec into the running 