{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "are you familiar with the bookfunnel.com author API?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, I’m familiar with BookFunnel’s Author API—it allows authors to generate unique download links or email readers directly from their own systems. Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 BookFunnel Author API Overview\n\n### 🔧 Base Setup\n- **Endpoint:** `https://api.bookfunnel.com/v1`\n- **Auth:** Send your API key in the `X-BookFunnel-APIKey` header (you can get one from the Integrations section in your BookFunnel account) citeturn0search0turn0search9.\n\n### 📚 Core API Endpoints\n\n1. **`/v1/create_book_link`**  \n   Generate a unique download link for a specific book.  \n   **Required params:**  \n   - `object`: Book ID (e.g., \"BF1234567890\")  \n   - `email`: Reader’s email  \n   **Returns:** JSON with `link` and `code` citeturn0search0.\n\n2. **`/v1/create_link`**  \n   Create a unique link for an opt-in landing page (without sending an email). Great if you’d like to handle the email flow yourself.  \n   **Returns similar JSON payload** citeturn0search0.\n\n3. **`/v1/email_link`**  \n   Generates a link *and* sends it to the reader via BookFunnel’s email. Optional params allow you to customize the subject, heading, message, and include the reader’s name citeturn0search0.\n\n4. **`/v1/test`**  \n   Validates your API key. Returns “Looks good!” along with your account name/email citeturn0search0.\n\n### 🌐 Webhooks (Optional)\nIf your plan includes integrations, BookFunnel supports webhooks—once enabled, you can receive:\n- `new_subscriber` (for opt-in landing pages)\n- `book_claimed` (for direct downloads) citeturn0search2  \n\nWebhook payload includes reader’s info, download link/code, IP, and page/book IDs.\n\n---\n\n## Example Workflow\n\n```http\nPOST /v1/create_book_link\nHeaders:\n  X-BookFunnel-APIKey: <your_api_key>\nBody (form-data):\n  object=BF1234567890\n  email=reader@example.com\n```\n\nResponse:\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"OK\",\n  \"message\": \"Link created.\",\n  \"link\": \"https://bookfunnel.com/XXX\",\n  \"code\": \"XYZ123\"\n}\n```\n\nYou can then deliver this link to the reader however you'd like—via your own email system or custom frontend.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Integrations\n\nBookFunnel also supports deep integration with WooCommerce, Payhip, Curios, and mailing platforms via direct API key setups or Zapier workflows citeturn0search4turn0search8turn0search10.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n- The API lets you generate and send custom download links for books and opt-in pages.\n- It’s authenticated via a single header.\n- Webhooks are available if you want realtime notifications of claims or subscriptions.\n- Recommended for anyone wanting to seamlessly integrate BookFunnel into custom workflows or storefronts.\n\nLet me know if you’d like help with usage examples, code snippets (like cURL, Node.js, PHP), or best practices!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYou **can** overlay or replace the \"GET FREE BOOK\" button (or landing page widget) with your own HTML/CSS/JS on your own site, but you **can’t directly modify the HTML on BookFunnel’s hosted pages**—those are managed on their servers.\n\n### Here are your options:\n\n---\n\n#### **1. On Your Own Site:**\nIf you want to **embed a BookFunnel opt-in or download link** on your website, you can create your own button, form, or fancy card that, when clicked, sends the user to your unique BookFunnel link. Example:\n\n```html\n<!-- Your custom button -->\n<a href=\"https://bookfunnel.com/your-unique-link\" class=\"my-free-book-btn\">\n  🎉 Get Your Free Book!\n</a>\n```\n\nYou can style this however you want (custom colors, icons, modals, animations, etc).\n\n---\n\n#### **2. BookFunnel Widget Embeds:**\nIf BookFunnel offers an **embed code** (some platforms offer JS widgets or iFrames for opt-ins), you can **wrap or style** the container on your site, but your ability to deeply customize what’s _inside_ the iFrame/widget is limited (iFrames are sandboxed for security).  \n*Check if their widget is customizable or just a static iFrame. If static, you’re limited to outer CSS only.*\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Direct API Integration:**\nSince you have API access, you could build your own:\n- Opt-in form (collect email, etc.)\n- Call the BookFunnel API from your backend to create/send the link\n- Display a custom “Success! Check your email!” message, etc.\n\nThis approach gives you **full creative control** and a seamless experience.\n\n---\n\n### **What You Can't Do:**\n\n- **You can’t** inject or override HTML on BookFunnel’s own hosted landing pages or pop-ups.  \n- **You can’t** “skin” their pages with your CSS/HTML (unless they specifically offer limited branding options via their dashboard).\n\n---\n\n### **Summary Table**\n\n| Scenario                          | Customizable?    | How?                          |\n|------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------|\n| **Your own site**                  | ✅ Fully         | Use your own HTML/CSS/JS      |\n| **BookFunnel widget/iFrame**       | ⚠️ Outer only    | Style wrapper, not inside     |\n| **BookFunnel’s hosted page**       | ❌ Not really    | Use their dashboard branding  |\n| **Direct API + your site**         | ✅ Fully         | Build your own flow           |\n\n---\n\n#### Want help with an API-powered “Get Free Book” flow that’s fully your own, with code examples? Or are you just looking for a simple HTML/CSS replacement for a button you embed on your site? Let me know how deep you want to go!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "What I'd like to do is overlay the HTML that they're using for \"GET FREE BOOK\" with my own, can do?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Absolutely! Here’s how you’d build a **custom “Get Free Book” flow** on your own website using BookFunnel’s Author API. This covers:\n\n1. **Frontend:** A form for the reader’s email (and maybe their name).\n2. **Backend:** Python Flask app that talks to BookFunnel’s API and returns a custom message or download link.\n3. **Optional:** Email delivery from your system, or redirect to the BookFunnel download page.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ **Frontend Example (HTML/JS)**\n\n```html\n<!-- index.html -->\n<h2>Get Your Free Book!</h2>\n<form id=\"get-book-form\">\n  <label>Email: <input type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required></label>\n  <button type=\"submit\">Claim Book</button>\n</form>\n<div id=\"response\"></div>\n\n<script>\ndocument.getElementById('get-book-form').onsubmit = async function(e) {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  let email = this.email.value;\n  document.getElementById('response').textContent = 'Processing...';\n  let resp = await fetch('/api/get-book', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n    body: JSON.stringify({ email })\n  });\n  let data = await resp.json();\n  if (data.link) {\n    document.getElementById('response').innerHTML = \n      `<a href=\"${data.link}\" target=\"_blank\">Download your book here!</a>`;\n  } else {\n    document.getElementById('response').textContent = data.message || 'Sorry, something went wrong.';\n  }\n};\n</script>\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ **Backend Example (Python Flask)**\n\n**Install dependencies:**  \n```bash\npip install flask requests\n```\n\n**app.py:**\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\nimport requests\n\nBOOKFUNNEL_API_KEY = 'YOUR_BOOKFUNNEL_API_KEY'\nBOOK_ID = 'YOUR_BOOKFUNNEL_BOOK_ID'  # like 'BFxxxxxxxxxx'\nBOOKFUNNEL_API_URL = 'https://api.bookfunnel.com/v1/create_book_link'\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/api/get-book', methods=['POST'])\ndef get_book():\n    data = request.get_json()\n    email = data.get('email', '').strip()\n    if not email:\n        return jsonify({'message': 'Email is required.'}), 400\n\n    resp = requests.post(\n        BOOKFUNNEL_API_URL,\n        headers={'X-BookFunnel-APIKey': BOOKFUNNEL_API_KEY},\n        data={'object': BOOK_ID, 'email': email}\n    )\n\n    if resp.status_code == 200:\n        res = resp.json()\n        if res.get('link'):\n            return jsonify({'link': res['link']})\n        else:\n            return jsonify({'message': res.get('message', 'No link returned.')})\n    else:\n        return jsonify({'message': 'Error connecting to BookFunnel.'}), 500\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ **How It Works**\n\n- **User submits email** on your branded form.\n- **Backend calls BookFunnel API** with that email & your book’s ID.\n- **Receives a unique download link** from BookFunnel.\n- **Frontend displays** a direct download link, or a “Check your email!” message if you configure BookFunnel to email the user directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ **Advanced Options**\n\n- **Collect Name:** Add a name field to your form and send it to BookFunnel if their API supports it.\n- **Double Opt-In:** Email the BookFunnel link from your own server (using your email template/branding).\n- **Analytics:** Log requests or trigger a webhook for your own CRM/newsletter list.\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ **Security/Spam Tips**\n\n- Throttle requests per IP/email to prevent abuse.\n- Validate emails before sending to BookFunnel.\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ **Custom Success Message**\n\nReplace the download link with your own thank-you page, or use BookFunnel’s email delivery instead of showing the link (by using `/v1/email_link`).\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 **Ready to Go?**\n\nLet me know if you want this in another framework (Django, Node, PHP), or want to tweak the flow (e.g., multi-book selection, opt-in + newsletter subscribe), or want production deployment advice!\n\n---\n\n**Just plug in your BookFunnel API key and Book ID and you’re good to go.**  \nWant a ZIP of this starter project or a deployable example?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Let's go with an API-powered “Get Free Book” flow that’s fully your own, with code examples, please."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "No problem! Here’s how you’d build a **custom “Get Free Book” API flow in Django**, using the BookFunnel Author API.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ **Django View (API Endpoint)**\n\nCreate a new Django app (let’s call it `bookfunnelapi`).  \nAdd this to `views.py`:\n\n```python\n# bookfunnelapi/views.py\n\nfrom django.views.decorators.csrf import csrf_exempt\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\nimport requests\nimport json\n\nBOOKFUNNEL_API_KEY = 'YOUR_BOOKFUNNEL_API_KEY'\nBOOK_ID = 'YOUR_BOOKFUNNEL_BOOK_ID'  # Example: 'BFxxxxxxxxxx'\nBOOKFUNNEL_API_URL = 'https://api.bookfunnel.com/v1/create_book_link'\n\n@csrf_exempt  # For simple demo; in production, set up proper CSRF token handling!\ndef get_book_link(request):\n    if request.method != 'POST':\n        return JsonResponse({'message': 'Invalid method.'}, status=405)\n\n    try:\n        data = json.loads(request.body)\n        email = data.get('email', '').strip()\n    except Exception:\n        return JsonResponse({'message': 'Invalid JSON.'}, status=400)\n\n    if not email:\n        return JsonResponse({'message': 'Email is required.'}, status=400)\n\n    resp = requests.post(\n        BOOKFUNNEL_API_URL,\n        headers={'X-BookFunnel-APIKey': BOOKFUNNEL_API_KEY},\n        data={'object': BOOK_ID, 'email': email}\n    )\n\n    if resp.status_code == 200:\n        res = resp.json()\n        if res.get('link'):\n            return JsonResponse({'link': res['link']})\n        else:\n            return JsonResponse({'message': res.get('message', 'No link returned.')})\n    else:\n        return JsonResponse({'message': 'Error connecting to BookFunnel.'}, status=500)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ **Add URL Route**\n\nIn `bookfunnelapi/urls.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom .views import get_book_link\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path('api/get-book/', get_book_link, name='get_book_link'),\n]\n```\n\nAnd in your project’s main `urls.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom django.urls import path, include\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    # ...your other URLs...\n    path('', include('bookfunnelapi.urls')),\n]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ **Frontend Form**\n\nYou can use the same HTML/JS as before.  \nPut this in your Django template (e.g., `templates/get_free_book.html`):\n\n```html\n<h2>Get Your Free Book!</h2>\n<form id=\"get-book-form\">\n  <label>Email: <input type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required></label>\n  <button type=\"submit\">Claim Book</button>\n</form>\n<div id=\"response\"></div>\n<script>\ndocument.getElementById('get-book-form').onsubmit = async function(e) {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  let email = this.email.value;\n  document.getElementById('response').textContent = 'Processing...';\n  let resp = await fetch('/api/get-book/', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n    body: JSON.stringify({ email })\n  });\n  let data = await resp.json();\n  if (data.link) {\n    document.getElementById('response').innerHTML = \n      `<a href=\"${data.link}\" target=\"_blank\">Download your book here!</a>`;\n  } else {\n    document.getElementById('response').textContent = data.message || 'Sorry, something went wrong.';\n  }\n};\n</script>\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ **Django Template View**\n\nAdd this to your `bookfunnelapi/views.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n\ndef get_book_page(request):\n    return render(request, 'get_free_book.html')\n```\n\nAnd add to `bookfunnelapi/urls.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom .views import get_book_page\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path('get-free-book/', get_book_page, name='get_book_page'),\n    path('api/get-book/', get_book_link, name='get_book_link'),\n]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ **Project Structure (Example)**\n\n```\nyourproject/\n├── bookfunnelapi/\n│   ├── views.py\n│   ├── urls.py\n│   └── templates/\n│       └── get_free_book.html\n├── yourproject/\n│   └── urls.py\n└── manage.py\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ **Production Tips**\n- Move your API key/Book ID into Django settings!\n- Use Django’s CSRF protection on the API endpoint in production.\n- Validate and sanitize user emails.\n- Style the form to match your branding.\n\n---\n\n### Ready to roll?  \nLet me know if you want a ZIP, a repo, or more advanced features (name fields, double opt-in, newsletter integration, etc)!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "I need a HTTP browser, one that doesn't do HTTPS by default because I'm trying to test my Docker installation on my laptop"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Got it — you're looking for a browser or tool that will allow you to browse **HTTP** endpoints without trying to upgrade to HTTPS (which almost all modern browsers will do by default).\n\nHere are a few options:\n\n### 🖥️ Option 1: Lightweight text browsers\nThese won’t auto-upgrade and are great for testing:\n\n- **w3m**  \n  ```bash\n  sudo apt install w3m\n  w3m http://localhost:8000\n  ```\n\n- **lynx**  \n  ```bash\n  sudo apt install lynx\n  lynx http://localhost:8000\n  ```\n\n- **links**  \n  ```bash\n  sudo apt install links\n  links http://localhost:8000\n  ```\n\nThese are **text browsers** but they respect your explicit HTTP.\n\n---\n\n### 🖥️ Option 2: Disable HTTPS upgrade in full browsers\n\n**Firefox** can be configured:\n1. Go to `about:config`\n2. Set:\n    - `dom.security.https_only_mode` → `false`\n    - `dom.security.https_first` → `false`\n\nThat will allow you to use `http://localhost:8000` without trying to switch to HTTPS.\n\n**Chromium / Chrome** — harder (they push HTTPS always), but you can launch it with flags:\n```bash\nchromium-browser --user-data-dir=/tmp/chromium-test --disable-features=UpgradeInsecureRequests\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🖥️ Option 3: Curl\nIf you're testing responses (not needing a rendered browser):\n```bash\ncurl -v http://localhost:8000\n```\n\n---\n\nIf you tell me **which OS you're on** and **which browser you normally use**, I can tailor this! Want me to prep a ready-to-go version for your laptop?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "WSL2. Curl is a possibility. All I need is to know that my server is sending data. Lynx or w3m might work, too (and better)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Perfect — for your use case (WSL2, just want to know that the server is sending data), both `curl` and `lynx` / `w3m` will work great.\n\nHere’s what you can do:\n\n### Option 1: Quick & simple with `curl`\n```bash\ncurl -v http://localhost:8000\n```\n- Shows full headers + body.\n- Great for “is my server up?”\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: Install & use `lynx` or `w3m` (better visual confirmation)\n\n**Install:**\n```bash\nsudo apt install lynx w3m\n```\n\n**Run lynx:**\n```bash\nlynx http://localhost:8000\n```\n\n**Run w3m:**\n```bash\nw3m http://localhost:8000\n```\n\n---\n\n**My recommendation** for WSL2 testing:  \n✅ Install both `curl` and `w3m` — fast, minimal, no GUI needed.\n\n---\n\nWant me to give you a one-liner to install both and test?  \nJust say: **“yes”** 🚀"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
