Field(unique=True)\n   ```\n2. Run:\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py makemigrations surveys\n   python manage.py migrate\n   ```\n\n---\n\n## **Summary Table**\n\n| Step         | What to Do                                   |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| 1            | Open shell                                   |\n| 2            | Print all surveys and slugs                  |\n| 3            | Use script to assign unique slugs to all     |\n| 4            | Print/verify: all slugs are unique, non-blank|\n| 5 (optional) | Make slug required & migrate                 |\n\n---\n\n**If you do all this and still get a unique constraint error, paste the output from step 2 (the list of survey slugs) here. I’ll debug instantly and show you exactly which rows need fixing!**\n\nYou're almost there. Let me know if you want a ready-to-paste migration for automating this!\n\n**Perfect,** that’s exactly right for this migration step:\n\n```python\nslug = models.SlugField(unique=True, null=True, blank=True)\n```\n\n**But:**  \nHaving the field like this in your model **doesn’t itself fix the UNIQUE constraint error unless every Survey in your database has a unique, non-blank slug value!**  \nDjango/SQLite may still fail if multiple Surveys have `slug=None` or `slug=''` (which both count as \"duplicate\" for the unique index in some databases).\n\n---\n\n## **Here’s Exactly What To Do**\n\n1. **Save your models.py** with the line above.\n2. **Run:**\n    ```bash\n    python manage.py makemigrations surveys\n    python manage.py migrate\n    ```\n   - If you get **no error**, proceed to step 4.\n   - If you still get the UNIQUE constraint error: go to step 3.\n\n3. **Open the Django shell:**\n    ```bash\n    python manage.py shell\n    ```\n    and run:\n    ```python\n    from surveys.models import Survey\n    from django.utils.text import slugify\n\n    # Set unique slugs for all surveys\n    for survey in Survey.objects.all():\n        