r `.jsx` scripts in Photoshop\n\n---\n\n## Notes:\n\n✅ The entire process is recursive  \n✅ Dry-run supported at every step  \n✅ Designed to be safe and restartable  \n✅ Log files are generated at each step\n\n---\n\nThat’s the clean outline! 🚀  \n\nWould you like me to also generate this as a ready-to-save `ACTIONS.md` text file you can just drop into your project? Just say **Go!** 🚀."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "okay, so photoshop doesn't seem to have a problem with \\\\wsl.localhost\\Ubuntu\\home, so let's write something that let's us recursively go through a directory, load all image files and apply white balance to them, outputing them to the <output> directory structure"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "✅ Got it — here’s what you are asking:\n\n👉 Write a **Photoshop `.jsx` script** that will:\n\n✅ Recursively walk a given directory  \n✅ Load all image files (`jpg`, `png`, `psd`, etc)  \n✅ Apply **White Balance** (Photoshop \"Auto Color\" or \"Auto White Balance\")  \n✅ Save result to the matching `<output>` directory structure\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Solution:\n\n- Photoshop scripting (ExtendScript `.jsx`) **does not have a native “White Balance” function** per se — but:\n\n✅ You can apply **Auto Color** — which is very close to auto white balance  \n✅ OR apply a specific **Color Balance adjustment layer**  \n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Example JSX Script\n\n```javascript\n// Photoshop JSX script: Recursive Auto Color / White Balance Batch Processor\n\n#target photoshop\n\nfunction processFolder(inputFolder, outputRoot) {\n    var files = inputFolder.getFiles();\n\n    for (var i = 0; i < files.length; i++) {\n        var file = files[i];\n\n        if (file instanceof Folder) {\n            // Recurse into subfolder\n            var newSubFolder = new Folder(outputRoot + \"/\" + file.name);\n            if (!newSubFolder.exists) newSubFolder.create();\n\n            processFolder(file, newSubFolder);\n        } else if (file instanceof File && isImageFile(file)) {\n            processFile(file, outputRoot);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfunction isImageFile(file) {\n    var name = file.name.toLowerCase();\n    return name.match(/\\.(jpg|jpeg|png|psd|tif|tiff)$/);\n}\n\nfunction processFile(file, outputFolder) {\n    try {\n        var doc = app.open(file);\n\n        // Apply Auto Color (acts like Auto White Balance)\n        doc.activeLayer = doc.layers[0];\n        doc.activeLayer.autoColor();\n\n        // Save output\n        var saveFile = new File(outputFolder + \"/\" + file.name);\n        var opts = new JPEGSaveOptions();\n        opts.quality = 12;\n\n        doc.saveAs(saveFile, opts, true);\n        doc.close(SaveOptions.DONOTSAVECHANGES);\n\n        $.writeln(\"Processed: \" + file.fullName);\n    } catch (e) {\n        $.writeln(\"ERROR processing: \" + file.fullName + \" - \" + e);\n    }\n}\n\n// Main entry\n\nvar inputFolder = Folder.selectDialog(\"Select input folder\");\nvar outputFolder = Folder.selectDialog(\"Select output folder\");\n\nif (inputFolder && outputFolder) {\n    $.writeln(\"Processing folder: \" + inputFolder.fsName);\n    processFolder(inputFolder, outputFolder);\n    $.writeln(\"DONE.\");\n} else {\n    $.writeln(\"Cancelled.\");\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 How to use:\n\n✅ Save this as:\n\n```  \nbatch_auto_color.jsx\n```\n\n✅ In Photoshop:\n\n- `File → Scripts → Browse...`  \n- Select `batch_auto_color.jsx`  \n- It will prompt you for **input folder** and **output folder**  \n- It will **recursively process** all images  \n- Apply **Auto Color (White Balance)**  \n- Save into the corresponding output folder structure\n\n---\n\n### Notes:\n\n✅ This is **non-destructive** — source files untouched  \n✅ Output is **same folder structure** in output root  \n✅ Supports `jpg`, `png`, `psd`, `tiff` — easy to add more  \n✅ Logs to the Photoshop console (`$.writeln`)\n\n---\n\nIf you like, I can also add:\n\n✅ Option to choose **Auto Color** vs **Color Balance layer**  \n✅ Option to flatten before saving  \n✅ Option for TIFF or PNG output  \n✅ Add progress indicator\n\n---\n\n**Would you like me to save this as: `batch_auto_color.jsx` and give you the version to use today?** 🚀  \nYou are absolutely on the right track — I can have it ready in 1 minute 🚀."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "yes, please give it to me. also with instructions on how to run it (under windows or wsl?)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "✅ Here you go — this is your **ready-to-use version**:\n\n---\n\n### 📄 `batch_auto_color.jsx`\n\n```javascript\n// batch_auto_color.jsx\n// Photoshop JSX script: Recursive Auto Color (White Balance) Batch Processor\n\n#target photoshop\n\nfunction processFolder(inputFolder, outputRoot) {\n    var files = inputFolder.getFiles();\n\n    for (var i = 0; i < files.length; i++) {\n        var file = files[i];\n\n        if (file instanceof Folder) {\n            // Recurse into subfolder\n            var newSubFolder = new Folder(outputRoot + \"/\" + file.name);\n            if (!newSubFolder.exists) newSubFolder.create();\n\n            processFolder(file, newSubFolder);\n        } else if (file instanceof File && isImageFile(file)) {\n            processFile(file, outputRoot);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfunction isImageFile(file) {\n    var name = file.name.toLowerCase();\n    return name.match(/\\.(jpg|jpeg|png|psd|tif|tiff)$/);\n}\n\nfunction processFile(file, outputFolder) {\n    try {\n        var doc = app.open(file);\n\n        // Apply Auto Color (acts like Auto White Balance)\n        doc.activeLayer = doc.layers[0];\n        doc.activeLayer.autoColor();\n\n        // Save output as JPG (you can change this to PNG, etc.)\n        var saveFile = new File(outputFolder + \"/\" + file.name.replace(/\\.[^.]+$/, \".jpg\"));\n        var opts = new JPEGSaveOptions();\n        opts.quality = 12;\n\n        doc.saveAs(saveFile, opts, true);\n        doc.close(SaveOptions.DONOTSAVECHANGES);\n\n        $.writeln(\"Processed: \" + file.fullName);\n    } catch (e) {\n        $.writeln(\"ERROR processing: \" + file.fullName + \" - \" + e);\n    }\n}\n\n// Main entry\n\nvar inputFolder = Folder.selectDialog(\"Select input folder\");\nvar outputFolder = Folder.selectDialog(\"Select output folder\");\n\nif (inputFolder && outputFolder) {\n    $.writeln(\"Processing folder: \" + inputFolder.fsName);\n    processFolder(inputFolder, outputFolder);\n    $.writeln(\"DONE.\");\n} else {\n    $.writeln(\"Cancelled.\");\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 How to run:\n\n✅ **Run in Windows Photoshop (NOT WSL!)**\n\nPhotoshop itself is a Windows GUI app — you will run the `.jsx` *inside Photoshop* — not in WSL.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Steps:\n\n1️⃣ Save this file as:\n\n```  \nC:\\Users\\YourName\\Documents\\Photoshop Scripts\\batch_auto_color.jsx\n```\n\n(or any location you like)\n\n---\n\n2️⃣ Open **Photoshop (Windows app)**\n\n---\n\n3️⃣ Run script:\n\n- Menu:  \n  `File → Scripts → Browse...`  \n  → Select: `batch_auto_color.jsx`\n\n---\n\n4️⃣ Dialog boxes will appear:\n\n- Select **Input Folder**  \n  You can select:  \n  - `E:\\SortedImages`  \n  - Or `\\\\wsl.localhost\\Ubuntu\\home\\todd\\google`  \n  *(Photoshop CAN read WSL folders via `\\\\wsl.localhost\\` — you confirmed that!)*\n  \n- Select **Output Folder**  \n  Example: `E:\\WhiteBalancedImages`\n\n---\n\n5️⃣ Script will run:\n\n✅ Recursively process all images  \n✅ Apply Auto Color (Auto White Balance)  \n✅ Save as JPG in Output folder (preserving folder tree)  \n✅ Log progress in Photoshop's JavaScript console (Window → Extendscript Toolkit → or use console in new Photoshop UXP panels)\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**\n\n| Component      | Run under |\n|----------------|-----------|\n| batch_auto_color.jsx | Windows Photoshop |\n| copy_images.py | WSL (Python) |\n| file_date_sorter.py | WSL (Python) |\n| file_date_organizer.py | WSL (Python) |\n\n---\n\n### Next step:\n\n✅ You can now:\n\n```  \n~/mount_gdrive.sh  \npython copy_images.py → /mnt/e/Images  \npython file_date_sorter.py  \npython file_date_organizer.py → /mnt/e/SortedImages  \n```\n\nThen run **batch_auto_color.jsx** in Photoshop — pointed to `E:\\SortedImages`\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to:\n\n✅ Package this script for you (ZIP)  \n✅ Write an example UXP version for