{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "File \"/home/todd/linux/src/shore/accounts/forms.py\", line 6, in <module>\n    class CustomUserCreationForm(UserCreationForm):\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/src/shore/accounts/forms.py\", line 7, in CustomUserCreationForm\n    class Meta:\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/src/shore/accounts/forms.py\", line 8, in Meta\n    model = CustomUser\n            ^^^^^^^^^^\nNameError: name 'CustomUser' is not defined"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That error is happening because your `CustomUserAdmin` class in `accounts/admin.py` still tries to **sort by the `username` field**, which no longer exists.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix: Update `CustomUserAdmin.ordering`\n\nIn `accounts/admin.py`, find your `CustomUserAdmin` definition, and change:\n\n#### ❌ Before (causing the error):\n```python\nordering = ['username']\n```\n\n#### ✅ After:\n```python\nordering = ['email']\n```\n\nOr if you want to order by creation time or ID:\n\n```python\nordering = ['id']  # or 'date_joined' if defined\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Bonus Cleanup (optional but recommended)\n\nAlso check your `list_display`, `search_fields`, or `fieldsets` — remove or replace `username` there too:\n\n#### For example:\n\n```python\nlist_display = ['email', 'is_staff']\nsearch_fields = ['email']\n```\n\nOnce updated, run your server again:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py runserver\n```\n\nLet me know if you’d like a complete clean version of `CustomUserAdmin`."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "<class 'accounts.admin.CustomUserAdmin'>: (admin.E033) The value of 'ordering[0]' refers to 'username', which is not a field of 'accounts.CustomUser'."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That line is most likely in your **`accounts/admin.py`** file, inside the `CustomUserAdmin` class.\n\nIt usually looks like this:\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom django.contrib.auth.admin import UserAdmin\nfrom .models import CustomUser\nfrom .forms import CustomUserCreationForm, CustomUserChangeForm\n\nclass CustomUserAdmin(UserAdmin):\n    model = CustomUser\n    add_form = CustomUserCreationForm\n    form = CustomUserChangeForm\n\n    list_display = [\"email\", \"is_staff\", \"is_active\"]\n    list_filter = [\"is_staff\", \"is_active\"]\n    ordering = [\"username\"]  # ❌ THIS IS THE LINE CAUSING THE ERROR\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix:\nChange:\n```python\nordering = [\"username\"]\n```\nto:\n```python\nordering = [\"email\"]\n```\nor:\n```python\nordering = [\"id\"]\n```\n\nLet me know if you'd like a cleaned-up `admin.py` fully adapted to your email-only user model."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "in which file is this: ordering = ['username']"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Thanks — I’ve checked your uploaded files, and to answer your question directly:\n\n### 🔎 **Where is `ordering = ['username']`?**\nNowhere in the files you uploaded — it's **not** present in your current `admin.py` file:\n\n```python\n# accounts/admin.py\nclass CustomUserAdmin(UserAdmin):\n    add_form = CustomUserCreationForm\n    form = CustomUserChangeForm\n    model = CustomUser\n    list_display = ['email', 'is_superuser']\n```\n\nSo the `ordering = ['username']` line must be coming from **Django’s default `UserAdmin`**, which your `CustomUserAdmin` is inheriting from.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix: Explicitly override the inherited `ordering`\n\nUpdate your `CustomUserAdmin` class to **add this line**:\n\n```python\nordering = ['email']  # ✅ or ['id'] if you'd prefer\n```\n\nFinal version:\n\n```python\nclass CustomUserAdmin(UserAdmin):\n    add_form = CustomUserCreationForm\n    form = CustomUserChangeForm\n    model = CustomUser\n    list_display = ['email', 'is_superuser']\n    ordering = ['email']  # ✅ this prevents the inherited ['username'] from causing errors\n```\n\nThen restart your dev server:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py runserver\n```\n\nLet me know if you’d like to strip `username` from any other places like filters or forms."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "where is that line occurring"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You're now seeing:\n\n> **`IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: accounts_customuser.username`**\n\nThis means the database still **has a `username` column defined as `NOT NULL`**, even though your model has `username = None`.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Why This Happens\n\nYou've correctly updated your `CustomUser` model:\n```python\nclass CustomUser(AbstractUser):\n    username = None\n    email = models.EmailField(unique=True)\n```\n\nBut your database still **expects a `username` column**, and it's marked `NOT NULL` — Django doesn’t auto-remove fields from existing tables when you remove them from models.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Solution Options\n\n#### ✅ Option 1: **Reset the database** (recommended for dev/test)\nIf you're still in development and can wipe your data:\n\n```bash\n# Optional: backup db.sqlite3 if needed\nrm db.sqlite3\npython manage.py migrate\n```\n\nThen re-create your superuser:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py createsuperuser\n```\n\nThis will fully apply your custom `User` model with no `username` field.\n\n---\n\n#### ✅ Option 2: **Create and apply a migration manually**\nIf you can’t wipe the database, you must:\n\n1. Comment out `username = None`\n2. Run `makemigrations`\n3. Manually edit the migration to remove the `username` field (tricky)\n4. Apply it with `migrate`\n\nThis is delicate and usually not worth it in early development.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Check\nAfter resetting, make sure:\n- You don’t refer to `username` in `forms.py`, `admin.py`, or templates\n- Allauth config is correct:\n  ```python\n  ACCOUNT_USERNAME_REQUIRED = False\n  ACCOUNT_USER_MODEL_USERNAME_FIELD = None\n  ACCOUNT_AUTHENTICATION_METHOD = 'email'\n  ```\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to give you a safe migration script to handle username removal, or should we go with the database reset?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "IntegrityError at /accounts/signup/\n\nNOT NULL constraint failed: accounts_customuser.username\n\nRequest Method: \tPOST\nRequest URL: \thttp://localhost:8000/accounts/signup/\nDjango Version: \t5.2.3\nException Type: \tIntegrityError\nException Value: \t\n\nNOT NULL constraint failed: accounts_customuser.username"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Based on your setup and the code you've shared, here are the most likely reasons your **welcome email didn’t get sent**, and how to check or fix them:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 1. **Signal or Hook Didn’t Trigger**\n\nYou've placed `send_welcome_email(user)` inside `save_user()` on both adapters:\n\n```python\nclass CustomAccountAdapter(DefaultAccountAdapter):\n    def save_user(self, request, user, form, commit=True):\n        user = super().save_user(request, user, form, commit)\n        self.send_welcome_email(user)\n        return user\n```\n\n#### 🔍 Did it trigger?\n- This only runs if **`CustomAccountAdapter`** is used during signup.\n- Check in `settings.py`:\n  ```python\n  ACCOUNT_ADAPTER = 'accounts.account_adapter.CustomAccountAdapter'\n  ```\n  ✅ This **is correctly set** per your earlier file.\n\n✅ So your adapter *should* be active.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 2. **Missing `send_welcome_email()` on One Adapter?**\nIn your **newer `adapter.py`**, the `CustomAccountAdapter` **does not have** the `send_welcome_email()` function.\n\nYou have two adapter files:\n- `account_adapter.py` ✅ includes `send_welcome_email()`\n- `adapter.py` ❌ does **not** — and might be the one actually in use if you changed the import path\n\n### 🔍 Check this:\nIn `settings.py`, confirm which adapter is **really** active:\n```python\nACCOUNT_ADAPTER = 'accounts.adapter.CustomAccountAdapter'\n```\nor:\n```python\nACCOUNT_ADAPTER = 'accounts.account_adapter.CustomAccountAdapter'\n```\n\nIf it’s the second one (`account_adapter.py`), you’re good.  \nIf it’s the first (`adapter.py`), you need to add `send_welcome_email()` there.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 3. **SMTP2GO misconfiguration**\n\nYou’re using:\n\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = \"django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend\"\nEMAIL_HOST = \"mail.smtp2go.com\"\nEMAIL_PORT = 587\nEMAIL_USE_TLS = True\nEMAIL_HOST_USER = config(\"EMAIL_HOST_USER\")\nEMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD = config(\"EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD\")\n```\n\n#### 🔍 Check:\n- Did you define the `EMAIL_HOST_USER` and `EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD` in your `.env` or environment variables?\n- Add a test email sender to verify delivery:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py shell\n```\n```python\nfrom django.core.mail import send_mail\nsend_mail(\"Test Email\", \"If you get this, SMTP is working.\", \"you@yourdomain.com\", [\"your_email@gmail.com\"])\n```\n\nIf this fails, it's an SMTP issue.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 4. **Template missing or error in template rendering**\n\nCheck if this file exists:\n```\ntemplates/emails/welcome_email.txt\n```\n\nAnd that it has no bad template tags (`{{ user.username }}` might fail now!).\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 5. **Check Email Logs / Debug Output**\n\nIn your view or `send_welcome_email()`, temporarily add:\n\n```python\nprint(\"Sending welcome email to\", user.email)\n```\n\nTo confirm it’s even being reached.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Summary Fix Checklist\n\n| Check | Fix |\n|-------|-----|\n| Is the right adapter used? | Make sure `ACCOUNT_ADAPTER` in settings matches the file with `send_welcome_email()` |\n| Template exists? | Confirm `templates/emails/welcome_email.txt` |\n| SMTP config working? | Test with `send_mail()` in shell |\n| Template uses correct field? | Use `{{ user.email }}` not `username` |\n| Print debug line? | Add `print()` in `send_welcome_email()` |\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to generate a minimal test to confirm adapter + SMTP are working together?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "why didn't my welcome email get sent"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Thanks — here’s how to **properly update your `adapter.py`** to include `send_welcome_email()` in both account and social signup paths.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Updated `adapter.py`\n\n```python\nfrom allauth.socialaccount.adapter import DefaultSocialAccountAdapter\nfrom allauth.account.adapter import DefaultAccountAdapter\nfrom django.core.mail import send_mail\nfrom django.template.loader import render_to_string\nfrom django.conf import settings\nimport uuid\n\n\nclass NoSignupFormAdapter(DefaultSocialAccountAdapter):\n    def is_open_for_signup(self, request, sociallogin):\n        return True\n\n    def is_auto_signup_allowed(self, request, sociallogin):\n        user = sociallogin.user\n        email = user.email or sociallogin.account.extra_data.get(\"email\")\n        return bool(email)\n\n    def send_welcome_email(self, user):\n        subject = \"🎉 Welcome to Foxxe Labs!\"\n        message = render_to_string(\"emails/welcome_email.txt\", {\"user\": user})\n        send_mail(\n            subject,\n            message,\n            settings.DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL,\n            [user.email],\n            fail_silently=False,\n        )\n\n    def save_user(self, request, sociallogin, form=None):\n        user = sociallogin.user\n        extra_data = sociallogin.account.extra_data\n\n        user.email = user.email or extra_data.get('email')\n        user.first_name = user.first_name or extra_data.get('given_name', '')\n        user.last_name = user.last_name or extra_data.get('family_name', '')\n        user.username = ''\n        user.is_active = True\n        user.save()\n\n        self.send_welcome_email(user)\n        return user\n\n\nclass CustomAccountAdapter(DefaultAccountAdapter):\n    def is_open_for_signup(self, request):\n        return True\n\n    def clean_username(self, username):\n        return str(uuid.uuid4())\n\n    def send_welcome_email(self, user):\n        subject = \"🎉 Welcome to Foxxe Labs!\"\n        message = render_to_string(\"emails/welcome_email.txt\", {\"user\": user})\n        send_mail(\n            subject,\n            message,\n            settings.DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL,\n            [user.email],\n            fail_silently=False,\n        )\n\n    def save_user(self, request, user, form, commit=True):\n        user = super().save_user(request, user, form, commit)\n        self.send_welcome_email(user)\n        return user\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Confirm These Settings\n\nIn `settings.py`, be sure you're pointing to this file:\n\n```python\nACCOUNT_ADAPTER = 'accounts.adapter.CustomAccountAdapter'\nSOCIALACCOUNT_ADAPTER = 'accounts.adapter.NoSignupFormAdapter'\n```\n\nAnd to confirm output while testing:\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = 'django.core.mail.backends.console.EmailBackend'\n```\n\nNow when any new user signs up — via **email or Google/social** — the welcome email will be printed to your console.\n\nLet me know if you want to add an HTML version or test this with real delivery later!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
