\", line 21, in provider_login_url\n    provider = adapter.get_provider(request, provider)\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/src/sandy/lib/python3.12/site-packages/allauth/socialaccount/adapter.py\", line 213, in get_provider\n    app = self.get_app(request, provider=provider, client_id=client_id)\n          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/src/sandy/lib/python3.12/site-packages/allauth/socialaccount/adapter.py\", line 303, in get_app\n    raise SocialApp.DoesNotExist()\nallauth.socialaccount.models.SocialApp.DoesNotExist\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 13 tests in 1.590s\n\nFAILED (errors=7)\nDestroying test database for alias 'default'..."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The error you're seeing:\n\n```\nIndentationError: expected an indented block after function definition on line 135\n```\n\nmeans that the function `create_test_google_social_app()` is defined, but there's **no code block inside** it — likely a copy/paste issue.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Fix\n\nMake sure the `create_test_google_social_app()` function is **indented properly and complete**.\n\nHere’s the correct version:\n\n```python\ndef create_test_google_social_app():\n    site = Site.objects.get_or_create(id=1, domain=\"localhost\", name=\"localhost\")[0]\n    app = SocialApp.objects.create(\n        provider=\"google\",\n        name=\"Google\",\n        client_id=\"test-client-id\",\n        secret=\"test-secret-key\",\n    )\n    app.sites.add(site)\n    return app\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🛠 Where to put it:\n\nPlace this anywhere near the top of `tests.py`, after your imports:\n\n```python\nfrom allauth.socialaccount.models import SocialApp\nfrom django.contrib.sites.models import Site\n```\n\n---\n\nOnce that's fixed, rerun your tests:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py test accounts\n```\n\nLet me know if you want the full corrected `tests.py` regenerated with all changes applied."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Found 1 test(s).\nSystem check identified no issues (0 silenced).\nE\n======================================================================\nERROR: accounts.tests (unittest.loader._FailedTest.accounts.tests)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nImportError: Failed to import test module: accounts.tests\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.12/unittest/loader.py\", line 394, in _find_test_path\n    module = self._get_module_from_name(name)\n             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/lib/python3.12/unittest/loader.py\", line 337, in _get_module_from_name\n    __import__(name)\n  File \"/home/todd/linux/src/shore/accounts/tests.py\", line 136\n    site = Site.objects.get_or_create(id=1, domain=\"localhost\", name=\"localhost\")[0]\n    ^^^^\nIndentationError: expected an indented block after function definition on line 135\n\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 1 test in 0.000s\n\nFAILED (errors=1)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You’ve done everything right so far — and you’re *very close* — but the remaining errors all stem from this:\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Root Cause\n\n```\ndjango.db.utils.IntegrityError: UNIQUE constraint failed: django_site.id\n```\n\nThis happens because you're trying to create a `Site` with `id=1`, **but it already exists** in the test database. When you specify `id=1` in `get_or_create()`, Django can't auto-increment — and you get a conflict.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Fix: Don’t specify the `id` in `get_or_create()`\n\n### 🔧 Update your `create_test_google_social_app()` to this:\n\n```python\ndef create_test_google_social_app():\n    site, _ = Site.objects.get_or_create(domain=\"localhost\", name=\"localhost\")\n\n    app, _ = SocialApp.objects.get_or_create(\n        provider=\"google\",\n        name=\"Google\",\n        client_id=\"test-client-id\",\n        secret=\"test-secret-key\",\n    )\n    app.sites.add(site)\n    return app\n```\n\nThis avoids the `UNIQUE constraint` error by letting Django assign the ID. It also makes your tests more portable and database-agnostic.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Also: Ensure this runs in `setUp()` for **every** test class that hits a template with `{% provider_login_url 'google' %}` or calls social views.\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\ndef setUp(self):\n    print(f\"\\nRunning tests in: {self.__class__.__name__}\")\n    create_test_google_social_app()\n```\n\nAdd that to:\n- `SignupTests`\n- `LoginLogoutTests`\n- `URLTests`\n- `GoogleSSOTests`\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Bonus Tip for Safety\n\nYou can use this helper to clean up and avoid `DoesNotExist` errors:\n\n```python\ndef ensure_social_app_exists():\n    if not SocialApp.objects.filter(provider=\"google\").exists():\n        create_test_google_social_app()\n```\n\nUse it if you want a quick check that doesn't duplicate records.\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you want me to regenerate the full `tests.py` with these updates built in — you're about one step away from green tests! ✅"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
