{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what about using get_template"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You're seeing:\n\n> `Your branch and 'origin/main' have diverged, and have 1 and 2 different commits each, respectively.`\n\nThat means:\n\n- You made **1 commit locally**.\n- The remote (`origin/main`) has **2 commits** that you don't have.\n- You've already committed your changes (`nothing to commit, working tree clean`), but haven't pushed or pulled yet.\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ To Resolve This\n\n#### ✅ 1. **First, fetch the latest from remote**\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\n```\n\n#### ✅ 2. **Then, check what’s different**\n```bash\ngit log --oneline --graph --all\n```\nThis shows you the commit history and where the divergence happened.\n\n#### ✅ 3. **Merge or Rebase**\n\nYou now have two main options:\n\n---\n\n### 🔀 Option 1: Merge (Simple, safe)\n```bash\ngit pull origin main\n```\n\nThis will create a **merge commit** combining your local changes with the remote.\n\n---\n\n### 🔄 Option 2: Rebase (Cleaner history)\n```bash\ngit pull --rebase origin main\n```\n\nThis will **replay your commit on top of the remote** — cleaner, but be careful if others are working on the branch.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ 4. **Push it up**\nOnce merged or rebased, push your branch:\n\n```bash\ngit push origin main\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you'd like a one-line script to automate this based on your preferred method (merge vs. rebase)."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
