{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what's the import line to load get_template"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "In Django, when you load a template using `get_template()`, the returned object is a `Template` instance (or a subclass like `Template` or `Engine.get_template()` output). It doesn't expose a ton of attributes, but here are the most useful ones:\n\n---\n\n### 🧱 Common Attributes of a Template Object\n\n#### ✅ `template.name`\n- The name or path of the template.\n- Example:\n  ```python\n  template = get_template('about.html')\n  print(template.name)  # 'about.html'\n  ```\n\n#### ✅ `template.origin`\n- Where the template was loaded from.\n- Can be useful for debugging.\n\n```python\nprint(template.origin.name)  # Full filesystem path to the template\n```\n\n#### ⚙️ `template.engine`\n- The underlying template engine used (typically `django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates`).\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Methods (More Commonly Used than Attributes)\n\n#### ✅ `template.render(context)`\n- Renders the template with a given context dictionary.\n- Example:\n  ```python\n  html = template.render({'user': 'Todd'})\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### 🔬 Example Inspection\n\n```python\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\ntemplate = get_template('about.html')\nprint(\"Template name:\", template.name)\nprint(\"Template origin:\", template.origin.name)\nprint(\"Template engine:\", type(template.engine))\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you want to inspect the parsed template nodes (advanced/debugging use)."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what are the attributes of a template"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great catch — you're seeing this error because `get_template()` in modern Django (when using the **DjangoTemplates** backend) returns a **`django.template.backends.django.Template`** object, which does *not* expose a `.name` attribute directly.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ How to Access Template Metadata (Alternative)\n\nTo get the **origin path** of the template (e.g. its file path), use:\n\n```python\nh = get_template('home.html')\nprint(h.template.name)           # Full path or template name\nprint(h.template.origin.name)    # Actual filesystem path\n```\n\nHere’s what’s going on:\n\n- `h` is a `BackendTemplate` wrapper.\n- `h.template` is the low-level `Template` object (from `django.template.base`).\n- `h.template.origin.name` gives the path of the loaded template.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Example from the Django shell:\n\n```python\n>>> from django.template.loader import get_template\n>>> h = get_template('home.html')\n>>> h.template.origin.name\n'/home/todd/linux/src/shore/pages/templates/pages/home.html'\n```\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Note\nThese attributes may not exist if the template was created from a string or isn't file-based, so wrap in `getattr` or `hasattr` if you're writing general-purpose tooling.\n\nLet me know if you want to inspect more details like the node list or parse tree."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
