{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "PSVersion says that I've got 5.1.26100 but I've installed Powershell 7.5.1, what gives?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great question — here's what's going on:\n\n### 🔍 Why you're seeing **5.1.26100** instead of **7.5.1**\n\nPowerShell **5.1** is **Windows PowerShell**, which is the legacy version **built into Windows**.\n\nPowerShell **7.x** (like your 7.5.1) is **PowerShell Core**, also known as **\"PowerShell 7\"**, and is **installed separately** — it's a **cross-platform, open-source** version.\n\n### ✅ So why are you getting 5.1?\n\nYou're probably launching **Windows PowerShell** (`powershell.exe`) instead of **PowerShell 7** (`pwsh.exe`).\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Here's how to fix it:\n\n#### Option 1: Run PowerShell 7 explicitly\n\nOpen a terminal and type:\n\n```powershell\npwsh\n```\n\nThat should launch PowerShell 7. Then run:\n\n```powershell\n$PSVersionTable\n```\n\nYou should now see:\n\n```\nPSVersion      7.5.1\n```\n\n#### Option 2: Make PowerShell 7 your default\n\n1. **Windows Terminal**:\n   - Open Windows Terminal\n   - Click the dropdown ▾ and select **Settings**\n   - Set **PowerShell 7** (`pwsh`) as the default profile\n\n2. **Taskbar shortcut**:\n   - Right-click the shortcut you're using\n   - Go to **Properties**\n   - Make sure the path points to `pwsh.exe`, usually:\n     ```\n     C:\\Program Files\\PowerShell\\7\\pwsh.exe\n     ```\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Summary\n\n| Version        | Name                | Executable  |\n|----------------|---------------------|-------------|\n| 5.1.xxxxx      | Windows PowerShell  | `powershell.exe` |\n| 7.x.x          | PowerShell (Core)   | `pwsh.exe`       |\n\nYou're seeing 5.1 because you're still running `powershell.exe`. Run `pwsh` instead to get into PowerShell 7.5.1.\n\nLet me know if you want to **replace the default shell globally**, or set up shortcuts or aliases."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
