ed in `INSTALLED_APPS`\n\nThen **Django will find those templates automatically**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ To ensure all app-level templates are found:\n\n1. ✅ Confirm all apps are listed in `INSTALLED_APPS`:\n   ```python\n   INSTALLED_APPS = [\n       ...\n       'accounts',\n       'pages',\n       ...\n   ]\n   ```\n\n2. ✅ Keep `'APP_DIRS': True` (you already have it — good!).\n\n3. ✅ Structure templates in each app like this:\n   ```\n   pages/\n     └── templates/\n           └── pages/\n                 └── base.html\n   accounts/\n     └── templates/\n           └── registration/\n                 └── login.html\n   ```\n\n   (Django will search inside `templates/` folders of each app listed in `INSTALLED_APPS`.)\n\n---\n\n### ❗ Optional (for project-wide templates):\n\nIf you want to use a **central `templates/` folder**, add it to `DIRS`:\n\n```python\n'DIRS': [BASE_DIR / 'templates'],\n```\n\nThen you can do this:\n```\nproject_root/\n  └── templates/\n        └── registration/\n              └── login.html\n```\n\nThis is useful for overriding built-in templates (like login) globally.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to restructure one of your apps for best practices, or confirm which setup is in place?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what's the difference between Django 5.0.14 and the latest version?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The latest stable release of Django as of **June 11, 2025** is **Django 5.2.3 (LTS)**, released June 10, 2025 citeturn0search0turn0search3.\n\nHere’s how Django 5.0.14 and Django 5.2.3 compare:\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ Security\n\n- **5.0.14** (released April 2, 2025) fixed a **moderate-severity** DoS vulnerability (CVE‑2025‑27556) affecting `LoginView`, `LogoutView`, and `set_language()` on Windows citeturn0search1turn0search17.\n- **5.2.3** includes this patch (and its variants) in the LTS branch, so using 5.2.3 means you're fully protected against it citeturn0search0turn0search17.\n\n---\n\n## 🔧 New Features in Django 5.2 (beyond 5.0.x)\n\nDjango 5.2 is an LTS (Long-Term Support) release with several new features over the 5.0 series:\n\n1. **Automatic model import in `shell`** – introspects apps and imports models automatically citeturn0search8turn0search2.  \n2. **Support for composite primary keys** – allows more complex primary-key setups citeturn0search8.  \n3. Inclusion of all security improvements from 5.1 and 5.0, including Windows DoS fix citeturn0search0turn0search17.\n\nYou can browse plenty more details in the 5.2 release notes citeturn0search2.\n\n---\n\n## ⏰ Support Status & Version Policy\n\n- **5.0** support ended **April 1, 2025** citeturn0search15turn0search6.  \n- **5.2** LTS will be actively supported until **December 2025**, and receive security updates through **April 2028** citeturn0search0turn0search6.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Recommendation\n\n- Stick with **5.2.3 (LTS)** for long-term, secure, stable support.\n- If you’re currently on **5.0.14**, upgrading to 5.2.3 is strongly recommended for continued maintenance, better support, and to access new features.\n\n---\n\n### TL;DR\n\n| Feature                          | Django 5.0.14 | Django 5.2.3 (Latest LTS) |\n|----------------------------------|----------------|------------------------------|\n| Supported & maintained           | ❌ (Ended Apr 2025) | ✅ (Support through Dec 2025; security until Apr 2028) |\n| Security vulnerability CVE‑2025‑27556 patched | ✅              | ✅                      |\n| Composite PK support in ORM      | ❌              | ✅                      |\n| Auto-import models in `shell`   | ❌              | ✅                      |\n\n---\n\nSo the main differences lie in **longer support**, **enhanced security**, and **useful new capabilities** in Django 5.2.3. Let me know if you'd like guidance on how to plan the migration!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what's the next planned version of Django and its releaase schedule"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The next feature release following Django 5.2 is **Django 6.0**, currently in active development. Here's the planned schedule 📅 based on official roadmap details:\n\n---\n\n### 🔜 Django 6.0 Timeline & Milestones\n\n- **Feature freeze**: set for **September 17, 2025**, meaning no new features can be merged after this point citeturn0search0turn0search7.\n- **Release candidate (RC)** and translation-string freeze typically follow about a month later, per Django's release process citeturn0search7.\n- **Final (stable) release**: targeted for **December 2025**, aligning with time-based feature release cadence citeturn0search0turn0search7.\n\n---\n\n### 🆕 Expected Features\n\nCore planned enhancements for 6.0 include:\n- **Built-in support for Django Tasks**, enabling easier background job scheduling.\n- **Integrated Content Security Policy (CSP)** support to improve security out-of-the-box citeturn0search0.\n\nMost major features have already begun development, and contributions through testing, reviews, and bug reporting are encouraged.\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ Release Process Overview\n\nDjango typically follows a structured release procedure:\n1. **Active development** → new features merged into `main`.\n2. **Feature freeze** → branch forked (`stable/6.0.x`).\n3. **Beta/RC phases** → focus on bug fixes and stability.\n4. **Strings freeze & final release** → translation lock, then stable rollout citeturn0search7.\n\nFeature releases occur roughly every 8 months, with 5.2 released April 2025 and 6.0 expected December 2025 citeturn0search3turn0search7.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Summary Table\n\n| Milestone              | Date              |\n|------------------------|--------------------|\n| Feature freeze         | Sep 17, 2025       |\n| Release candidate(s)   | ~Oct – Nov 2025    |\n| Final release          | Dec 2025           |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 What This Means for You\n\n- **Planning to upgrade?** Start preparing now—especially if considering adoption around beta or RC stages.\n- **Contributing?** Explore the `main` branch to test new features or assist in the review process.\n- **Need stability?** Django 5.2 remains LTS until December 2025, making it a safe production choice through 6.0's launch.\n\nIf you're curious about migration steps, backward-incompatibilities, or testing strategies, feel free to ask!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what's going to be in the 6.0 release"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here’s what’s shaping up for **Django 6.0**, expected in **December 2025**:\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Key New Features & Enhancements\n\n**1. Python Support Update**  \n- Drops Python 3.10‑3.11 support—only Python 3.12 and 3.13 are officially supported. citeturn0search0\n\n**2. django.contrib.admin**  \n- Updated to use **Font Awesome Free 6.7.2** icons. citeturn0search0\n\n**3. Password Security**  \n- In `django.contrib.auth`, PBKDF2 default hashing rounds increase from 1,000,000 to **1,200,000** for enhanced security. citeturn0search0\n\n**4. GIS Enhancements**  \n- `GEOSGeometry.hasm` property checks for 4D geometries.  \n- New `Rotate` DB function allows geometric rotations. citeturn0search0\n\n**5. ORM & Aggregation Improvements**  \n- `AnyValue`, `StringAgg`, and support for `order_by` in aggregates.  \n- `QuerySet.raw()` now works with composite primary keys.  \n- `JSONField` on SQLite supports negative indexing.  \n- `Model.save()` may raise `Model.NotUpdated` if an update ends up affecting no rows. citeturn0search0\n\n**6. Async Pagination**  \n- New `AsyncPaginator` and `AsyncPage` types for native async support. citeturn0search0\n\n**7. Templates & Querystring Tag**  \n- Adds `forloop.length` variable.  \n- `querystring` tag now reliably prefixes `?` and supports multiple maps. citeturn0search0\n\n**8. Migrations & Management**  \n- Squashed migrations can be re-squashed.  \n- `ZoneInfo` serialization is supported.  \n- `startproject/startapp` will auto-create target dirs if missing. citeturn0search0\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Backwards-Incompatible Changes\n\n- **MariaDB 10.5 support dropped**; minimum is 10.6. citeturn0search0turn0search12  \n- `BaseDatabaseCreation.create_test_db(serialize)` deprecated—use `serialize_db_to_string()`.  \n- Database backends no longer use `CASCADE` on column drops. citeturn0search0\n\n---\n\n## 🗓️ Release Timeline\n\nHere's the planned Django 6.0 release schedule:\n\n- **Feature freeze (alpha)**: Sep 17, 2025  \n- **Beta 1**: Oct 22, 2025  \n- **Release candidate (RC1)**: Nov 19, 2025  \n- **Final release**: Dec 3, 2025 citeturn0search3\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Summary: What 6.0 Means for You\n\n- **Modernized core**: Python 3.12+ only, with elevated security standards.  \n- **ORM & Query improvements**: Better aggregation, full composite PK compatibility, JSON support.  \n- **Async-friendly**: Async pagination, improved template tools.  \n- **Dropped legacy & refined support**: Cleaner DB backend assumptions and reduced technical debt.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ What to Do Next\n\n1. **Upgrade Python** to 3.12 or 3.13.  \n2. **Audit DB compatibility**—drop MariaDB 10.5, update custom backends.  \n3. **Test new features** like async paginator and template enhancements.  \n4. **Check deprecations** ahead of migration to catch breaking changes early.\n\nLet me know if you’d like help planning your upgrade, exploring specific features, or assessing compatibility!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "walk me through how to create a new git repo from the command line"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Sure! Here's a step-by-step guide to creating a new Git repository from the command line.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Step-by-Step: Create a New Git Repository\n\n### 🧱 1. Create a Project Directory (if needed)\n```bash\nmkdir my-new-project\ncd my-new-project\n```\n\n### 🧬 2. Initialize a Git Repository\n```bash\ngit init\n```\nThis creates a `.git/` folder which holds all the version control metadata.\n\n### 📄 3. Add Some Files\nCreate files manually or use a command:\n```bash\ntouch README.md\necho \"# My Project\" > README.md\n```\n\n### 📦 4. Stage Your Files\nAdd files to the staging area:\n```bash\ngit add .\n```\nOr selectively:\n```bash\ngit add README.md\n```\n\n### 📝 5. Commit the Files\nCommit your changes with a message:\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"Initial commit\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Optional: Connect to a Remote Repository (e.g. GitHub)\n\n### Step 1: Create a new empty repo on GitHub/GitLab (no README, no .gitignore)\n\n### Step 2: Add the remote\n```bash\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/my-new-project.git\n```\n\n### Step 3: Push to the remote\n```bash\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n> If your default branch is `master`, use that instead of `main`.\n\n---\n\n## 🧪 Verify Everything\n```bash\ngit status      # Check current status\ngit remote -v   # Verify the remote is set correctly\n```\n\n---\n\nWould you like a GitHub repo automatically created via the GitHub CLI (`gh`), or want help configuring SSH instead of HTTPS?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "git remote add https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\nusage: git remote add [<options>] <name> <url>\n\n    -f, --[no-]fetch      fetch the remote branches\n    --[no-]tags           import all tags and associated objects when fetching\n                          or do not fetch any tag at all (--no-tags)\n    -t, --[no-]track <branch>\n                          branch(es) to track\n    -m, --[no-]master <branch>\n                          master branch\n    --[no-]mirror[=(push|fetch)]\n                          set up remote as a mirror to push to or fetch from"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You're seeing that error because the `git remote add` command requires **two arguments**: a **remote name** and a **URL**.\n\nYou probably ran:\n\n```bash\ngit remote add https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n```\n\nBut the correct format is:\n\n```bash\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n```\n\n### ✅ Correct Command:\n```bash\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n```\n\n- `origin` is the name you're giving to the remote (conventionally used for the main remote repo).\n- `https://github.com/todd427/shore.git` is the remote repository URL.\n\nAfter adding the remote, you can verify it:\n```bash\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nLet me know if you'd like to use SSH instead of HTTPS for authentication or if you want to push to the remote next."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "git push -u origin main\nerror: src refspec main does not match any\nerror: failed to push some refs to 'https://github.com/todd427/shore.git'"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The error:\n\n```\nerror: src refspec main does not match any\n```\n\nmeans Git can't find a local branch named `main`.\n\nThis usually happens because:\n- You haven't made any commits yet.\n- Or your branch is named `master`, not `main`.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Fix in 3 Quick Steps\n\n### 🔧 1. Check your current branch\n```bash\ngit branch\n```\nIf it shows:\n```\n* master\n```\n…then you're on `master`, not `main`.\n\n### 💾 2. Make your first commit (if you haven’t)\nIf you haven’t made any commits yet, Git won’t push anything.\n\n```bash\necho \"# Shore\" > README.md\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m \"Initial commit\"\n```\n\n### 🚀 3. Push using the correct branch name\n\nIf you're on `master`:\n```bash\ngit push -u origin master\n```\n\nIf you *do* want your branch to be called `main` instead, you can rename it:\n\n```bash\ngit branch -m main\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you'd like help renaming or aligning branches with GitHub’s defaults!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "cannot find repo shore on my account on git"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "If you're getting errors like:\n\n> `fatal: repository 'https://github.com/todd427/shore.git/' not found`\n\nIt likely means the repo **hasn't been created yet on GitHub**, or you're pointing to the wrong URL.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Fix: Create the GitHub Repo First\n\nHere’s how to do it step-by-step:\n\n### 🧭 1. Go to [GitHub.com](https://github.com) and log in\n\n### ➕ 2. Click the \"+\" icon → **\"New repository\"**\n\n- **Repository name**: `shore`\n- Choose **Public** or **Private**\n- Leave **README unchecked** (you already have one locally)\n- Click **\"Create repository\"**\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 3. Update Your Remote (if needed)\n\nGitHub will show you the correct remote URL. Run:\n\n```bash\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n```\n\nIf you already added the wrong remote earlier, update it like this:\n\n```bash\ngit remote set-url origin https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 4. Push Your Code\n\n```bash\ngit push -u origin main\n```\nOr, if your branch is called `master`:\n```bash\ngit push -u origin master\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Optional: Verify Everything\n\n```bash\ngit remote -v\ngit branch\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you'd prefer using SSH, or want to automate repo creation with the `gh` CLI!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "git remote add origin https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\nerror: remote origin already exists."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That error means you've **already added a remote called `origin`**.\n\nTo fix this, you have two options:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Option 1: Update the Existing Remote\n\nIf `origin` points to the wrong URL (or you want to ensure it's correct), just reset it:\n\n```bash\ngit remote set-url origin https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Option 2: Check What It's Currently Set To\n\nRun this to inspect your current remote:\n\n```bash\ngit remote -v\n```\n\nOutput might look like:\n```\norigin  https://github.com/todd427/something-else.git (fetch)\norigin  https://github.com/todd427/something-else.git (push)\n```\n\nIf it's wrong, update it using the `set-url` command above.\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Push After That\n\nNow you can push:\n\n```bash\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\nOr, if your branch is named `master`:\n\n```bash\ngit push -u origin master\n```\n\nLet me know what you see from `git remote -v` or if you're still not seeing the repo on GitHub."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "git push -u origin main\nerror: src refspec main does not match any\nerror: failed to push some refs to 'https://github.com/todd427/shore.git'"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You're seeing this error because **your local Git repo doesn't have a branch named `main` yet**, or it has **no commits**, which means there's no `main` to push.\n\nLet’s walk you through fixing this cleanly:\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Step-by-Step Fix\n\n### 🔍 1. Check Your Current Branch\n```bash\ngit branch\n```\nYou’ll probably see:\n```\n* master\n```\nSo your branch is named `master`, not `main`.\n\n---\n\n### 🧱 2. Make Sure You Have at Least One Commit\n\nIf you haven’t committed anything yet, Git has nothing to push. Let’s fix that:\n\n```bash\necho \"# Shore\" > README.md\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m \"Initial commit\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 3. Push the Correct Branch\n\nNow push your current branch (`master`) to GitHub:\n\n```bash\ngit push -u origin master\n```\n\n✅ That should work!\n\n---\n\n### 🔁 (Optional) Rename `master` to `main`\n\nIf you want your branch to follow the `main` naming convention:\n\n```bash\ngit branch -m main              # Rename locally\ngit push -u origin main         # Push it to GitHub\ngit symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD refs/remotes/origin/main  # optional default set\n```\n\nThen delete `master` on GitHub if it was auto-created:\n```bash\ngit push origin --delete master\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you want a clean `main` setup from scratch or want to sync with an existing GitHub branch!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "git push origin --delete master\nTo https://github.com/todd427/shore.git\n ! [remote rejected] master (refusing to delete the current branch: refs/heads/master)\nerror: failed to push some refs to 'https://github.com/todd427/shore.git'"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That error means GitHub is refusing to delete the `master` branch because **it's currently set as the default branch** for the repository.\n\nGitHub doesn’t let you delete the default branch via Git commands until you change the default in the web UI.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Fix: Change Default Branch on GitHub\n\nHere’s how:\n\n### 🔧 1. Go to your repo on GitHub  \n👉 [https://github.com/todd427/shore](https://github.com/todd427/shore)\n\n### ⚙️ 2. Click on **Settings** (⚙️ tab at the top of the repo)\n\n### 🌿 3. Under **\"Branches\"**, find the **default branch** section\n\n- Click **\"Change default branch\"**\n- Select `main` from the dropdown\n- Confirm the change\n\n---\n\n## 🗑️ 4. Now Delete the Old `master` Branch\n\nOnce `main` is the default, you can delete `master`:\n\n```bash\ngit push origin --delete master\n```\n\n✅ That should now succeed.\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you’d like help automating this flow via the GitHub CLI or handling local cleanup afterward."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "let's setup .gitignore"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great move — a well-crafted `.gitignore` keeps your repo clean and safe from junk and secrets.\n\n---\n\n## 🧱 Basic Python + Django `.gitignore` Template\n\nCreate a `.gitignore` file in your project root:\n\n```bash\ntouch .gitignore\n```\n\nThen add the following content:\n\n```gitignore\n# Byte-compiled / cache\n__pycache__/\n*.py[cod]\n*.pyo\n*.pyd\n*.so\n\n# Virtual environment\nvenv/\n.env/\n*.env\n\n# Django stuff\n*.log\n*.pot\n*.pyc\ndb.sqlite3\nmedia/\nstaticfiles/\n*.sqlite3\n\n# VS Code and IDEs\n.vscode/\n.idea/\n*.sublime-project\n*.sublime-workspace\n\n# OS-specific\n.DS_Store\nThumbs.db\n\n# Migrations (optional if you regenerate often)\n**/migrations/__pycache__/\n**/migrations/*.pyc\n# **/migrations/*.py  <-- Uncomment to ignore all migration files\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Save and Add to Git\n\n```bash\ngit add .gitignore\ngit commit -m \"Add .gitignore for Python/Django project\"\n```\n\n---\n\nWould you like to:\n- Ignore compiled frontend files too? (`node_modules/`, `dist/`, etc.)\n- Include project-specific patterns?\n- Automatically generate one based on your tech stack via [gitignore.io](https://www.toptal.com/developers/gitignore)?\n\nLet me know how tailored you want it!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "how do i delete from my repo"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "To delete files **from your Git repo** (both locally and on GitHub), follow one of the methods below depending on what you want to remove:\n\n---\n\n## 🔥 To Delete a File (and commit the removal)\n\n```bash\ngit rm path/to/file\ngit commit -m \"Remove file from repo\"\ngit push\n```\n\n> This removes the file from both your working directory **and** the Git history (once pushed).\n\n---\n\n## 🧽 To Delete a File **only from the repo** but keep it locally\n\nUseful for sensitive files you want to **ignore going forward** (e.g., `.env`, `settings_local.py`):\n\n```bash\ngit rm --cached path/to/file\necho \"path/to/file\" >> .gitignore\ngit commit -m \"Stop tracking path/to/file\"\ngit push\n```\n\n> Now GitHub will forget about it, but the file stays on your disk and gets ignored in future commits.\n\n---\n\n## 🗃️ To Delete a Directory from the Repo\n\n```bash\ngit rm -r path/to/directory\ngit commit -m \"Remove directory from repo\"\ngit push\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Quick Check\n\nAfter any delete:\n```bash\ngit status         # Shows what changed\ngit log -- path/to/file  # See commit history of that file\n```\n\n---\n\nTell me what you want to delete and whether you want to **keep it locally** — I’ll tailor the exact command!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "can you write tests for this"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Thanks! Based on your `models.py`, `forms.py`, and other files, you're using a **custom user model** (`CustomUser`) and two associated forms: `CustomUserCreationForm` and `CustomUserChangeForm`.\n\nLet’s write tests for:\n\n1. **Model behavior** (`CustomUser`)\n2. **Form validation** (`CustomUserCreationForm` and `CustomUserChangeForm`)\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Create a Test File\n\nInside your Django app (e.g. `accounts/`), create `tests.py` if it doesn't exist:\n\n```bash\ntouch accounts/tests.py\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🧪 Sample Test Cases\n\nPaste the following into `accounts/tests.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom django.test import TestCase\nfrom django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\nfrom .forms import CustomUserCreationForm, CustomUserChangeForm\n\nUser = get_user_model()\n\nclass CustomUserModelTests(TestCase):\n    def test_create_user(self):\n        user = User.objects.create_user(\n            username=\"testuser\",\n            email=\"test@example.com\",\n            password=\"securepass123\"\n        )\n        self.assertEqual(user.username, \"testuser\")\n        self.assertEqual(user.email, \"test@example.com\")\n        self.assertTrue(user.check_password(\"securepass123\"))\n\n    def test_create_superuser(self):\n        superuser = User.objects.create_superuser(\n            username=\"admin\",\n            email=\"admin@example.com\",\n            password=\"adminpass\"\n        )\n        self.assertTrue(superuser.is_superuser)\n        self.assertTrue(superuser.is_staff)\n\nclass CustomUserCreationFormTests(TestCase):\n    def test_valid_form(self):\n        form_data = {\n            \"username\": \"newuser\",\n            \"email\": \"newuser@example.com\",\n            \"password1\": \"StrongPass123\",\n            \"password2\": \"StrongPass123\",\n        }\n        form = CustomUserCreationForm(data=form_data)\n        self.assertTrue(form.is_valid())\n\n    def test_password_mismatch(self):\n        form_data = {\n            \"username\": \"newuser\",\n            \"email\": \"newuser@example.com\",\n            \"password1\": \"pass1\",\n            \"password2\": \"pass2\",\n        }\n        form = CustomUserCreationForm(data=form_data)\n        self.assertFalse(form.is_valid())\n        self.assertIn(\"password2\", form.errors)\n\nclass CustomUserChangeFormTests(TestCase):\n    def test_change_email(self):\n        user = User.objects.create_user(\n            username=\"testuser\",\n            email=\"old@example.com\",\n            password=\"somepass\"\n        )\n        form_data = {\n            \"username\": \"testuser\",\n            \"email\": \"new@example.com\",\n        }\n        form = CustomUserChangeForm(instance=user, data=form_data)\n        self.assertTrue(form.is_valid())\n        updated_user = form.save()\n        self.assertEqual(updated_user.email, \"new@example.com\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Run the Tests\n\nIn your terminal, run:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py test accounts\n```\n\n---\n\nWould you like to add tests for views or authentication flows next (e.g., login/signup)?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
