use the fully qualified name**:\n\n```\n'courses:course_edit'\n```\n\n### ✅ How to Fix\n\nWherever you're using `{% url 'course_edit' pk=course.id %}`, update it to:\n\n```django\n{% url 'courses:course_edit' pk=course.id %}\n```\n\nOr in Python code:\n\n```python\nreverse('courses:course_edit', kwargs={'pk': course.id})\n```\n\n### 🔍 Follow-up\n\nIf you're still getting this error after making that change, share the line from the template or view that uses `course_edit`, and I can give you the exact correction.\n\n\n\nThe new error:\n\n```\nReverse for 'manage_course_list' not found. 'manage_course_list' is not a valid view function or pattern name.\n```\n\n…is essentially the **same issue as before**: you’re trying to reverse a view name (`manage_course_list`) **without including the namespace**, even though your `urls.py` file declares:\n\n```python\napp_name = 'courses'\n```\n\nSo again, **you must prefix all URL names with the namespace** `courses`.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix for DeleteView\n\nIn your `CourseDeleteView`, you likely have something like this:\n\n```python\nsuccess_url = reverse_lazy('manage_course_list')\n```\n\nThat should be:\n\n```python\nfrom django.urls import reverse_lazy\n\nsuccess_url = reverse_lazy('courses:manage_course_list')\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fix in Templates (if applicable)\n\nIf you're using `{% url 'manage_course_list' %}` in your templates, update it to:\n\n```django\n{% url 'courses:manage_course_list' %}\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you'd like me to review the `CourseDeleteView` class or any specific template that includes the redirect!"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
