port fork-based multiprocessing used by Celery’s default `prefork` pool.**\n\n---\n\n## ✅ **Solution: Use the `solo` pool on Windows**\n\nYou must run Celery using the `--pool=solo` option.\n\n### 🔧 Fix it like this:\n\nRun the worker with:\n\n```bash\ncelery -A myshop worker --pool=solo -l info\n```\n\nOr if you're using PowerShell in a virtual environment:\n\n```powershell\n(myshop) PS E:\\src\\myshop> celery -A myshop worker --pool=solo -l info\n```\n\nThis switches from multiprocessing to a **single-threaded event loop** that works safely on Windows.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Optional: Use Redis Instead of RabbitMQ (Better Windows compatibility)\n\nRabbitMQ can be fragile on Windows. Redis is often easier for development.\n\nTo use Redis:\n1. Install Redis:  \n   Download from https://github.com/tporadowski/redis/releases (Windows-native Redis)\n\n2. Start Redis server\n\n3. Set broker URL in your Django settings:\n\n```python\nCELERY_BROKER_URL = 'redis://localhost:6379/0'\n```\n\nThen run Celery:\n\n```bash\ncelery -A myshop worker --pool=solo -l info\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Summary\n\n| Action | Command |\n|--------|---------|\n| Fix multiprocessing crash | `--pool=solo` |\n| Use Redis instead of RabbitMQ | Set `CELERY_BROKER_URL = 'redis://localhost:6379/0'` |\n| Want multiprocessing? | Use Linux or WSL2 |\n\n---\n\nWould you like help switching to Redis or setting up Celery with Django step-by-step?"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
