{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "ssl.SSLCertVerificationError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Basic Constraints of CA cert not marked critical (_ssl.c:1028)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Thanks for the error. You're seeing:\n\n```\nssl.SSLCertVerificationError: [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: Basic Constraints of CA cert not marked critical (_ssl.c:1028)\n```\n\nThis typically means that Python (or Django via Python) is trying to validate an SSL certificate for your email server, but the **certificate authority (CA) used by the server has an invalid or non-standard configuration**—specifically, the \"Basic Constraints\" extension in the CA certificate is not marked `critical`, which it should be according to stricter SSL clients.\n\n### 🔍 Likely causes\n1. **You're using a custom or self-signed certificate** (common in dev/staging environments).\n2. **Your Python environment doesn't trust the CA** or has outdated root certs.\n3. **The email server is misconfigured**, using a non-compliant certificate.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Solutions\n\n#### ✅ 1. **For development (bypass verification — not recommended for production)**\n\nYou can use a custom email backend that disables SSL verification. This is a hack and should *only* be used for **development**:\n\n```python\n# myapp/email_backend.py\nimport smtplib\nfrom django.core.mail.backends.smtp import EmailBackend\n\nclass InsecureEmailBackend(EmailBackend):\n    def _get_connection(self):\n        connection = super()._get_connection()\n        if hasattr(connection, 'sock'):\n            connection.sock = connection.sock\n        if hasattr(connection, 'starttls'):\n            context = self._create_ssl_context()\n            context.check_hostname = False\n            context.verify_mode = smtplib.ssl.CERT_NONE\n            connection.starttls(context=context)\n        return connection\n```\n\nThen in `settings.py`:\n\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = 'myapp.email_backend.InsecureEmailBackend'\n```\n\n> ⚠️ This bypasses certificate verification — okay for dev/testing, never for production.\n\n---\n\n#### ✅ 2. **For production (fix the certificate issue)**\n\n- **Check your mail provider's documentation** — make sure you're connecting to the correct host with a **valid TLS certificate**.\n- If you're self-hosting email (e.g., Postfix, Sendmail, custom SMTP), make sure your certificate:\n  - Is signed by a trusted CA\n  - Has proper `Basic Constraints` marked `critical`\n\n---\n\n#### ✅ 3. **Update root certificates in your Python environment**\n\nIf you're on macOS and using the built-in Python:\n\n```bash\n/Applications/Python\\ 3.x/Install\\ Certificates.command\n```\n\nIf you're on Linux, update certs:\n\n```bash\nsudo update-ca-certificates\n```\n\nIf using `certifi` (e.g., with `requests`, which `send_mail` might be using under the hood):\n\n```bash\npip install --upgrade certifi\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🛠️ Django Email Setup Checklist (for context)\n\nIn `settings.py`:\n\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = 'django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend'\nEMAIL_HOST = 'smtp.yourmailserver.com'\nEMAIL_PORT = 587\nEMAIL_USE_TLS = True\nEMAIL_HOST_USER = 'your@email.com'\nEMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD = 'your_password'\n```\n\n---\n\nIf you're unsure where the failure is happening, try this basic test using Python directly:\n\n```python\nimport smtplib\nserver = smtplib.SMTP('smtp.yourmailserver.com', 587)\nserver.starttls()  # Likely where the cert error occurs\nserver.login('your@email.com', 'your_password')\nserver.sendmail('from@example.com', 'to@example.com', 'Test message')\nserver.quit()\n```\n\nLet me know your mail provider and whether you're using dev/prod — I can give you a tailored fix."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "I'm using smtp.gmail.com and I'm dev"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Perfect — since you're using Gmail (`smtp.gmail.com`) in **development**, here's how to get past that `ssl.SSLCertVerificationError` safely **without disabling all SSL checks globally or permanently**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Best Quick Fix for Development\n\nUpdate your Django `settings.py` like this:\n\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = 'django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend'\nEMAIL_HOST = 'smtp.gmail.com'\nEMAIL_PORT = 587\nEMAIL_USE_TLS = True\nEMAIL_HOST_USER = 'your_email@gmail.com'\nEMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD = 'your_app_password'  # NOT your Gmail password!\n```\n\n#### ❗ Step 1: Use an App Password (not your Gmail password)\nIf you haven’t already:\n\n1. Go to [https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords](https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords)\n2. Select \"Mail\" > \"Other (Custom name)\" and generate an app password\n3. Use that app password in `EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD`\n\n> 🔒 Google blocks less-secure sign-ins, and this is the safe, supported way to use Gmail with Django.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Why you’re still getting SSL errors in dev\n\nSome Python installations (especially on macOS or older Linux) **don’t trust all CAs** used by Gmail out of the box.\n\nTo fix that:\n\n#### ✅ macOS: Run the cert fix script (if you installed Python from python.org)\n\n```bash\n/Applications/Python\\ 3.x/Install\\ Certificates.command\n```\n\n#### ✅ Linux: Update your system certs\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install --reinstall ca-certificates\nsudo update-ca-certificates\n```\n\n#### ✅ Windows (if using `requests`, `certifi`, or `pip` errors)\n\nUpdate Python and/or the `certifi` package:\n\n```bash\npip install --upgrade certifi\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Still failing? Temporarily bypass SSL check (dev only!)\n\nIf you’ve exhausted all options and want a **temporary workaround**:\n\n```python\n# myapp/email_backend.py\nimport ssl\nfrom django.core.mail.backends.smtp import EmailBackend\n\nclass UnsafeGmailBackend(EmailBackend):\n    def open(self):\n        if self.connection:\n            return False\n        try:\n            self.connection = self.connection_class(\n                self.host, self.port, **self.connection_params\n            )\n            context = ssl._create_unverified_context()  # bypass SSL cert check\n            self.connection.starttls(context=context)\n            self.connection.login(self.username, self.password)\n            return True\n        except Exception:\n            if not self.fail_silently:\n                raise\n```\n\nThen in `settings.py`:\n\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = 'myapp.email_backend.UnsafeGmailBackend'\n```\n\n> ⚠️ **This disables SSL verification. DO NOT use in production.**\n\n---\n\nLet me know your OS and Python version if you're still stuck — I’ll give you an exact fix."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "what is the server sendmail syntax"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The basic syntax to send an email using the `sendmail` command on a Unix/Linux server is:\n\n```bash\nsendmail recipient@example.com\n```\n\nYou typically pass the message content to `sendmail` via standard input (stdin), including headers like `To:`, `From:`, and `Subject:`. Here's a minimal example:\n\n### ✅ Example (interactive or script)\n```bash\nsendmail recipient@example.com <<EOF\nTo: recipient@example.com\nFrom: you@example.com\nSubject: Test Email from Sendmail\n\nThis is the body of the email.\nEOF\n```\n\n### ✅ Example from a file:\nCreate a file `message.txt`:\n\n```\nTo: recipient@example.com\nFrom: you@example.com\nSubject: Hello from Sendmail\n\nThis is a test message sent from the server using sendmail.\n```\n\nThen run:\n\n```bash\nsendmail recipient@example.com < message.txt\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Notes:\n\n- `sendmail` must be installed and properly configured (e.g. via Postfix or Exim).\n- Use full headers (`To`, `From`, `Subject`) or the email may be marked as spam.\n- You can send to multiple recipients by listing them:\n  ```bash\n  sendmail user1@example.com user2@example.com\n  ```\n\n---\n\nIf you're scripting this in Python or want to wrap it from Django (not recommended unless you control the server mailer), let me know and I can help you do that too."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nAttributeError: 'UnsafeGmailBackend' object has no attribute 'connection_params'. Did you mean: 'connection_class'?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Great catch — that error:\n\n```\nAttributeError: 'UnsafeGmailBackend' object has no attribute 'connection_params'. Did you mean: 'connection_class'?\n```\n\n…means the code I gave referenced an internal Django SMTP backend attribute (`self.connection_params`) that doesn’t exist (or isn’t accessible in that way). Let's fix that with a working version of a custom Django `EmailBackend` that disables SSL certificate verification **only for development**, using `starttls()`.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Corrected Custom Backend (DEV-ONLY)\n\n```python\n# myapp/email_backend.py\nimport ssl\nimport smtplib\nfrom django.core.mail.backends.smtp import EmailBackend\n\nclass UnsafeGmailBackend(EmailBackend):\n    def open(self):\n        if self.connection:\n            return False\n\n        try:\n            # Create the actual SMTP connection\n            self.connection = smtplib.SMTP(self.host, self.port, timeout=self.timeout)\n\n            # Bypass SSL verification\n            context = ssl._create_unverified_context()\n            self.connection.starttls(context=context)\n\n            if self.username and self.password:\n                self.connection.login(self.username, self.password)\n\n            return True\n        except Exception:\n            if not self.fail_silently:\n                raise\n```\n\nThen, in your `settings.py`:\n\n```python\nEMAIL_BACKEND = 'myapp.email_backend.UnsafeGmailBackend'\n\nEMAIL_HOST = 'smtp.gmail.com'\nEMAIL_PORT = 587\nEMAIL_USE_TLS = True  # still required to trigger starttls\nEMAIL_HOST_USER = 'your_email@gmail.com'\nEMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD = 'your_app_password'  # generated from https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🛑 Warning (again): This skips SSL verification!\n\nThis should only be used:\n- In local development environments\n- When you're encountering SSL errors due to local cert issues\n- Temporarily\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you'd like a safer way to work with Gmail that keeps verification intact, or if you'd like to fully switch to using `sendmail` or another backend."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "cannot access local variable 'form' where it is not associated with a value"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The error:\n\n```\nUnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'form' where it is not associated with a value\n```\n\n…means you're trying to use a variable (`form`) before it's been assigned — usually due to a conditional path in your view not assigning it in all branches.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Common Django View Example That Triggers This\n\n```python\ndef contact_view(request):\n    if request.method == 'POST':\n        form = ContactForm(request.POST)\n        if form.is_valid():\n            # Do something\n            return redirect('success')\n    # ❌ If request.method is not POST, `form` is never defined here!\n    return render(request, 'contact.html', {'form': form})\n```\n\nThis throws the error on `render(...)` because `form` is only defined in the `POST` branch.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Corrected Version\n\n```python\ndef contact_view(request):\n    if request.method == 'POST':\n        form = ContactForm(request.POST)\n        if form.is_valid():\n            # Process form\n            return redirect('success')\n    else:\n        form = ContactForm()  # ✅ now defined for GET and POST\n\n    return render(request, 'contact.html', {'form': form})\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 TL;DR\n\nWhenever you use a variable in a view (or any function), make sure it’s defined in **all execution paths**. For Django forms, it's typical to initialize an empty form in the `GET` branch and a populated form in `POST`.\n\nIf you show me your full view code, I’ll patch it for you."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "def post_share(request, post_id):\n    # Retrieve post by id\n    post = get_object_or_404(Post, id=post_id, status=Post.Status.PUBLISHED)\n    sent = False\n    if (request.method == 'POST'):\n        # Form was submitted\n        form = EmailPostForm(request.POST)\n        if (form.is_valid()):\n            # Form fields passed validation\n            cd = form.cleaned_data\n            post_url = request.build_absolute_uri(post.get_absolute_url())\n            subject = f\"{cd['name']} recommends you read {post.title}\"\n            message = f\"Read {post.title} at {post_url}\\n\\n{cd['name']}\\'s comments: {cd['comments']}\"\n            send_mail(subject, message, from_email=None, recipient_list=[cd['to']])\n            sent = True\n        else:\n            form = EmailPostForm()\n    return render(\n        request,\n        'blog/post/share.html',\n         {'post': post, 'form': form, 'sent': sent}\n         )"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The problem in your view is here:\n\n```python\nelse:\n    form = EmailPostForm()\n```\n\nThis `else` is nested under `if form.is_valid()`, **not** under `if request.method == 'POST'`. So when `request.method != 'POST'`, `form` is never defined — and you get the error:\n\n```\nUnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'form' where it is not associated with a value\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Fixed Version\n\nMove the `else:` block up to match `if request.method == 'POST'`:\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.mail import send_mail\nfrom django.shortcuts import render, get_object_or_404\nfrom .models import Post\nfrom .forms import EmailPostForm\n\ndef post_share(request, post_id):\n    post = get_object_or_404(Post, id=post_id, status=Post.Status.PUBLISHED)\n    sent = False\n\n    if request.method == 'POST':\n        form = EmailPostForm(request.POST)\n        if form.is_valid():\n            cd = form.cleaned_data\n            post_url = request.build_absolute_uri(post.get_absolute_url())\n            subject = f\"{cd['name']} recommends you read {post.title}\"\n            message = f\"Read {post.title} at {post_url}\\n\\n{cd['name']}'s comments: {cd['comments']}\"\n            send_mail(subject, message, from_email=None, recipient_list=[cd['to']])\n            sent = True\n    else:\n        form = EmailPostForm()  # ✅ form defined for GET requests\n\n    return render(\n        request,\n        'blog/post/share.html',\n        {'post': post, 'form': form, 'sent': sent}\n    )\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Summary\n\n- You must define `form` regardless of whether the request is `POST` or `GET`.\n- Always check that variables used in `render()` are assigned for **all paths** through the function.\n\nLet me know if you want the matching template too."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "create five facebook ad campaigns to promote the Third Pass Pern books of the Dragonriders of Pern seris"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here are **five distinct Facebook ad campaign concepts** to promote the **Third Pass** books in *The Dragonriders of Pern* series. Each campaign targets different reader motivations—nostalgia, adventure, worldbuilding, character, and epic stakes.\n\n---\n\n### 📣 **CAMPAIGN 1: “Return to Pern” – For Nostalgic Fans**\n**🎯 Target Audience:** Adults 35–65, fantasy readers, original McCaffrey fans, Tolkien/Sanderson readers  \n**🎨 Creative:** Image of a majestic bronze dragon silhouetted against a Red Star sky, tagline overlay  \n**📝 Primary Text:**  \n💫 *Before F'lar. Before Lessa. Before the Weyrs were abandoned.*  \nReturn to the Third Pass—where Thread first fell and dragons truly earned their wings.\n\n📘 Dive into Pern’s earliest legends. Rediscover the wonder that started it all.\n\n🔥 *The Dragonriders of Pern: The Third Pass*. Your next great re-read starts here.\n\n**🔘 CTA:** [Read the Origin]  \n**🔗 Destination:** Amazon or dedicated landing page with Third Pass reading order\n\n---\n\n### 🛡️ **CAMPAIGN 2: “When Thread First Fell” – Action & Stakes**\n**🎯 Target Audience:** Sci-fi/fantasy adventure lovers, fans of military sci-fi or survival epics  \n**🎨 Creative:** Burning Thread falling from a red sky; dragonriders scrambling to mount  \n**📝 Primary Text:**  \nThread is coming.  \nPern has dragons—but not enough riders.  \nAnd no one is ready.\n\n⚔️ Witness the Third Pass—the first real fight for Pern’s survival.  \nDragons. Firestone. Fate.\n\n🪶 *Written by Anne & Todd McCaffrey.*\n\n**🔘 CTA:** [Join the Fight]  \n**🔗 Destination:** Series bundle or “Start Here” page\n\n---\n\n### 👩‍🚀 **CAMPAIGN 3: “Dragons Meet Sci-Fi” – For Cross-Genre Appeal**\n**🎯 Target Audience:** Sci-fi readers, fans of *Dune*, *Foundation*, or *The Expanse*  \n**🎨 Creative:** Split-frame image: futuristic colony ship on one side, dragon flight on the other  \n**📝 Primary Text:**  \n📡 A spacefaring colony.  \n🐉 A telepathic bond.  \n☄️ An alien threat no science could stop.\n\n*The Third Pass of Pern* is where high fantasy meets hard science fiction.  \nRediscover the genre-defying legacy of Anne McCaffrey.\n\n**🔘 CTA:** [Explore Pern’s Origins]  \n**🔗 Destination:** Blog or article on “Why Pern Still Matters”\n\n---\n\n### 🧬 **CAMPAIGN 4: “The First Weyrs” – For Worldbuilders & Lore Fans**\n**🎯 Target Audience:** Worldbuilding nerds, D&D players, fantasy wiki readers  \n**🎨 Creative:** Ancient map of Pern with old Weyr markings; burning thread lines traced  \n**📝 Primary Text:**  \nHow were the Weyrs founded?  \nWhat secrets did the Ancients leave behind?\n\nUnravel the early lore of Pern in the **Third Pass novels**, where dragons were fewer and the danger greater.\n\nPerfect for lore-lovers, worldbuilders, and anyone who wants to go deeper.\n\n**🔘 CTA:** [Uncover Pern’s Past]  \n**🔗 Destination:** Annotated guide or book 1 of Third Pass\n\n---\n\n### 👩‍🦰 **CAMPAIGN 5: “One Girl. One Egg. One Future.” – Character Hook**\n**🎯 Target Audience:** YA crossover readers, fans of Eragon, Aragon, or Tamora Pierce  \n**🎨 Creative:** Close-up of a young woman’s face lit by a dragon egg glowing red  \n**📝 Primary Text:**  \nShe didn’t ask for a dragon.  \nBut the egg chose her.  \nAnd now, the skies of Pern may depend on her courage.\n\n🖋 *Anne & Todd McCaffrey’s Third Pass novels* bring the magic of dragonriding to a new generation.\n\n**🔘 CTA:** [Meet Her Dragon]  \n**🔗 Destination:** First book featuring a strong young protagonist\n\n---\n\nWould you like these bundled as a downloadable campaign brief (PDF or DOCX)? I can also mock up sample graphics or write headlines and primary text variations for A/B testing."}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
{"system": "", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "mock up sample graphics, write headlines and primary text for A/B testing"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\n  \"prompt\": \"A fantasy-themed Facebook ad graphic featuring a bronze dragon soaring through a red-tinged sky above a rocky, alien landscape. Below, a banner reads: 'The Third Pass Has Begun – Discover Pern’s Origins'. Include subtle stars in the background, dramatic lighting, and a sense of epic scale. Ideal for a science-fantasy book series. No text overlays other than the banner. Style: Cinematic, epic, dramatic lighting.\",\n  \"size\": \"1024x1024\"\n}\n\n{\n  \"prompt\": \"Facebook ad graphic for a fantasy novel campaign titled 'Return to Pern'. The image features a majestic bronze dragon soaring through a crimson sky with the Red Star visible in the background. The terrain below is rugged, with the faint glow of Weyrs nestled in mountains. Text overlay in elegant serif font: 'Return to Pern – When Thread First Fell'. Moody, epic, and nostalgic color palette with golden highlights on the dragon.\",\n  \"size\": \"1024x1024\"\n}\n\nHere are three headline and primary text variations for A/B testing, based on the ad image:\n\n---\n\n### 🔠 **HEADLINE VARIANTS**\n**A.** Return to Pern  \n**B.** Before F’lar. Before Lessa. Discover Pern’s Origins  \n**C.** The Red Star Rises—And So Does a New Legend\n\n---\n\n### 📝 **PRIMARY TEXT VARIANTS**\n**1.**  \nThe Third Pass. When dragons were few and Thread was new.  \nRediscover the beginnings of the Weyrs in this epic chapter of *The Dragonriders of Pern*.\n\n**2.**  \nYou know the legends of Benden Weyr.  \nNow learn how they began—when firestone first blazed and dragons earned their place in the skies.\n\n**3.**  \nThread is falling.  \nThe first time, and no one is ready.  \nDive into *The Third Pass*—where Pern’s greatest story begins.\n\n---\n\nWould you like mockups for the other four campaigns too?\n\nHere are A/B test headline and primary text variants to pair with the “RETURN TO PERN” visual:\n\n---\n\n### 🔠 **Headline Variants**\n**A1:** Return to Pern  \n**A2:** Where Thread First Fell  \n**A3:** The Battle That Started It All  \n**A4:** Before the Weyrs Were Abandoned  \n**A5:** Rediscover the Legend of the Third Pass\n\n---\n\n### 📝 **Primary Text Variants**\n**B1:**  \nBefore F'lar. Before Lessa. Before the Weyrs were abandoned—there was the Third Pass.  \nThe skies burned. The dragons rose.  \nDiscover how it all began.\n\n**B2:**  \nWhat came before the legends?  \nThread is falling again—and Pern is unprepared.  \nStep into the world of the Third Pass.\n\n**B3:**  \nThe Red Star has returned.  \nOnly dragons can save Pern—but not all will survive.  \nRead the epic origins of the Dragonriders.\n\n**B4:**  \nThe Third Pass:  \nThe moment when Pern became legend.  \nWhen dragons flew. When riders were forged.  \nRelive the fire.\n\n**B5:**  \nThink you know Pern?  \nThink again.  \nThis is where the legacy began—when dragons earned their wings.\n\n---\n\nWould you like matching ad graphics for the other four campaigns too?\n\nYou're absolutely right — thank you for the correction.\n\n📘 **THREAD FIRST FELL** during the **First Pass**, shortly after the colonists settled on Pern.\n\n📘 The **Third Pass** is significant because:\n- It's the **first return of Thread after the Second Interval** (which lasted ~200 years),\n- It marks a time when the **Weyrs are under strain**, the **Healers are developing**, and the **hold-crafthall system is still evolving**,\n- It introduces new challenges, like **Plague**, **Healer Hall struggles**, and **leadership transitions** among Weyrs and Holds.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ REVISED CAMPAIGN HEADLINE + COPY (for \"Return to Pern\")\n\n**🖼️ Headline:**  \n“Return to Pern”\n\n**📝 Primary Text (Updated):**  \nBefore F’lar. Before Lessa. Before the world was ready...  \nThread returned. And the dragonriders rose again.\n\n🔥 *The Third Pass of Pern* is a time of rebirth, crisis, and fire.  \nFollow the bold men and women who shaped the Weyrs and saved a world still finding its footing.\n\n📚 Discover the *Dragonriders of Pern: The Third Pass* novels by Anne & Todd McCaffrey.\n\n**🔘 CTA:** [Read the Origin]\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to regenerate the graphic image with the corrected tagline and a new subtitle like:  \n> “Return to the Third Pass—where dragons rose again and Pern was tested anew”?\n\n{\n  \"prompt\": \"A dramatic fantasy poster for 'Dragonriders of Pern: The Third Pass.' A bronze dragon soars across a twilight sky, the Red Star glowing ominously in the background. The dragon is detailed and majestic, its wings outstretched in flight. Text overlays include: \\n\\nTOP: 'Before F’lar. Before Lessa. Before the world was ready...'\\nCENTER: 'RETURN TO PERN'\\nBOTTOM: 'Return to the Third Pass—where dragons rose again and Pern was tested anew.'\\nSUBTEXT: 'THE DRAGONRIDERS OF PERN: THE THIRD PASS'\\nCTA BUTTON: 'READ THE ORIGIN'\\n\\nDesign style: classic fantasy novel cover, moody lighting, epic tone.\",\n  \"size\": \"1024x1536\"\n}"}], "tags": ["chatgpt", "v1"]}
